-- [Foramen Ovale & Ductus Arteriosus] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A near-term fetus has not yet taken its first breath, so its lungs are compressed and fluid-filled, and the pulmonary vascular bed offers very high resistance to flow. As a result, most of the blood ejected from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk never reaches the lungs and instead ends up in the aorta. Which fetal shunt is responsible for this particular diversion?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The ductus arteriosus, which connects the pulmonary trunk directly to the aorta."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The foramen ovale, which connects the pulmonary trunk directly to the aorta."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The foramen ovale, which connects the right atrium directly to the left atrium."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The ductus venosus, which connects the umbilical vein directly to the inferior vena cava."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' Because the fetal lungs are compressed and nonfunctional, blood follows the path of least resistance away from the high-resistance pulmonary bed, and three shunts route it accordingly: the foramen ovale moves blood between the atria, the ductus arteriosus moves blood between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, and the ductus venosus lets placental blood bypass the liver. Identifying the correct shunt requires matching each vessel to the exact pair of structures it connects. (Choice A) is correct: the textbook states the ductus arteriosus is "a short, muscular vessel that connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta," and that "most of the blood pumped from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk is thereby diverted into the aorta" — exactly the flow the stem describes, driven by the high resistance of the collapsed pulmonary bed. (Choice B) is incorrect: it names the correct pulmonary-trunk-to-aorta connection but assigns it to the wrong structure; the foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum, not a vessel between the great arteries. (Choice C) is incorrect: this accurately describes the foramen ovale, which "allows blood to flow from the right atrium to the left atrium," but that shunt bypasses the lungs at the atrial level and does not handle blood already ejected into the pulmonary trunk. (Choice D) is incorrect: the ductus venosus truly connects the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava, letting oxygenated placental blood bypass the fetal liver, so it is unrelated to diverting pulmonary-trunk blood into the aorta. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the resistance principle and the distinct connections of each shunt to determine which one accounts for the described pulmonary-trunk-to-aorta flow, rather than recalling a single stated fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'e07f8f08-33c9-40c1-a46c-a278bca18221';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e07f8f08-33c9-40c1-a46c-a278bca18221';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e07f8f08-33c9-40c1-a46c-a278bca18221', 'B', 'misconception', 'correct relationship attributed to the wrong structure'),
  ('e07f8f08-33c9-40c1-a46c-a278bca18221', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'true statement about a different shunt that does not fit the described flow'),
  ('e07f8f08-33c9-40c1-a46c-a278bca18221', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'true statement about the third shunt that bypasses the liver, not the lungs');

-- [Twins] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher tracks a fertilized sea-star egg through the first several hours of development, recording the number of cells and the overall diameter of the embryo at each time point. She finds that the cell count rises from 1 to 2 to 4 to 8, yet the overall diameter of the embryo stays essentially unchanged across all of these time points. Which conclusion about this stage of development is best supported by her measurements?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The cells are dividing by mitosis but not growing between divisions, so each cell is progressively smaller than the one before it."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The cells are dividing by mitosis and also growing between divisions, so the embryo will soon expand as the divisions continue."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The cells are pausing between divisions to accumulate cytoplasm, so the overall diameter reflects growth that has stalled temporarily."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The cells are dividing by meiosis rather than mitosis, so each daughter cell carries half the genetic material of the original egg."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' The earliest stage of animal development is cleavage, a rapid series of mitotic divisions that partitions the zygote into progressively smaller cells called blastomeres. Cleavage is distinctive because the egg is subdivided "into smaller and smaller cells, with no actual cell growth," so the 8-cell stage and the later blastula are "about the same size as the original zygote" (L33222, L33242). (Choice A) is correct: constant overall diameter combined with a rising cell count means the fixed volume of the egg is being carved into ever-smaller cells by mitosis without any intervening growth, exactly the defining feature of cleavage. (Choice B) is incorrect: if the cells grew between divisions as ordinary somatic cells do, total volume and therefore diameter would increase with each round, which contradicts the observed unchanged diameter. (Choice C) is incorrect: cleavage divisions occur rapidly with little pause and involve no cytoplasmic accumulation, so invoking stalled growth misreads a defining property of cleavage as a temporary delay. (Choice D) is incorrect: cleavage proceeds by mitosis, which keeps each blastomere genetically identical and diploid; meiosis and halved genetic content occur in gamete formation, not in the developing embryo. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of cleavage—mitotic division without cell growth—to interpret data showing rising cell number at constant embryo size.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '9d67e148-b092-4ba1-a99a-34ef653a358f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9d67e148-b092-4ba1-a99a-34ef653a358f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9d67e148-b092-4ba1-a99a-34ef653a358f', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes embryonic divisions include growth like ordinary somatic mitosis'),
  ('9d67e148-b092-4ba1-a99a-34ef653a358f', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats constant size as stalled/paused growth rather than growth-free division'),
  ('9d67e148-b092-4ba1-a99a-34ef653a358f', 'D', 'misconception', 'confuses embryonic mitotic cleavage with meiosis');

-- [Foramen Ovale & Ductus Arteriosus] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A pregnant patient begins labor several weeks before her due date. To delay delivery, a physician administers a drug that competitively blocks oxytocin receptors on the smooth muscle of the uterine wall. Before treatment, the patient''s cervix was stretching and contractions were steadily increasing in force. Which outcome should the physician most expect after the drug takes effect, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Contractions weaken and labor slows, because the self-reinforcing cycle that escalates uterine contractions can no longer amplify itself."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Contractions become stronger and more frequent, because the body compensates by driving oxytocin output back up to its target level."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Contractions continue at the same escalating pace, because prostaglandins from the placenta sustain the cascade independently of oxytocin."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Contractions surge suddenly and uncontrollably, because blocking the receptors removes the inhibitory brake on uterine smooth muscle."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' Labor is driven by a positive feedback loop: stretching of the cervix triggers oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary, oxytocin binds receptors on uterine smooth muscle to stimulate contraction, and the resulting stretch drives still more oxytocin release, so contractions escalate until birth. The textbook states that in this system, "Instead of lowering the oxytocin, more oxytocin is produced until the contractions are powerful enough to produce childbirth," and that "Contractions increase in intensity as blood levels of oxytocin rise via a positive feedback mechanism until the birth is complete." (Choice A) is correct: because the loop is self-reinforcing, interrupting oxytocin''s action at its receptor breaks the link between contraction-driven stretch and further contraction, so the cascade can no longer amplify and contractions weaken. (Choice B) is incorrect: it treats parturition as a negative feedback loop that defends a set point, but positive feedback amplifies the stimulus rather than opposing deviations, so blocking the receptor does not trigger a compensatory rebound in contractile force. (Choice C) is incorrect: although the placenta releases prostaglandins that increase contractions, they act alongside the oxytocin loop rather than replacing it, so removing oxytocin''s action does not leave the escalating cascade intact. (Choice D) is incorrect: oxytocin is stimulatory, not inhibitory, so its receptor is not a brake; blocking a stimulatory signal reduces contraction rather than releasing a surge. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of positive feedback to a novel pharmacological intervention and predict how interrupting the loop changes the system, rather than recalling a stated fact.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '699d7390-3911-4daf-933f-485bfb0ff368';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '699d7390-3911-4daf-933f-485bfb0ff368';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('699d7390-3911-4daf-933f-485bfb0ff368', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'positive-feedback-mistaken-for-negative-feedback'),
  ('699d7390-3911-4daf-933f-485bfb0ff368', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'prostaglandin-role-overstated-as-oxytocin-independent'),
  ('699d7390-3911-4daf-933f-485bfb0ff368', 'D', 'misconception', 'stimulatory-signal-mistaken-for-inhibitory-brake');

-- [Gestation & Birth] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During established labor, a physician administers a drug that selectively blocks oxytocin receptors on uterine smooth muscle, leaving cervical stretch receptors and the hypothalamic response intact. Assuming no other intervention, what is the most likely immediate effect on the uterine contractions?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The contractions fail to build in intensity and labor stalls, because the amplifying loop driving them is interrupted."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The contractions continue at their previous intensity, because rising hormone release compensates for the blocked receptors."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The contractions proceed largely unchanged, because placental prostaglandins are what actually drive the uterine muscle."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The contractions abruptly strengthen, because removing this signal releases the uterus from its usual inhibition."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' During labor, cervical stretching signals the hypothalamus to trigger oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary; oxytocin makes the uterine smooth muscle contract, which stretches the cervix further and drives still more oxytocin release. Because each contraction increases the stimulus for the next, this is a positive feedback loop that amplifies contractions until birth, rather than a negative feedback loop that would hold output at a set point. (Choice A) is correct: oxytocin acting on its uterine receptors is the effector step of the positive feedback loop, so blocking those receptors breaks the amplifying cycle; contractions can no longer intensify and labor stalls (the clinical basis for oxytocin-blocking tocolytics). (Choice B) is incorrect: it treats the loop as negative (homeostatic) feedback that self-corrects toward a set point. Even if the hypothalamus releases more oxytocin, that oxytocin cannot act on blocked receptors, so contractions are not maintained. (Choice C) is incorrect: placental prostaglandins do augment contractions, but the text presents oxytocin acting on the uterus as the central driver of the positive feedback relay, so blocking oxytocin''s receptors still profoundly weakens contractions rather than leaving them unchanged. (Choice D) is incorrect: it reverses oxytocin''s role, confusing it with the quiescence-promoting effect of progesterone; oxytocin stimulates rather than inhibits the uterine muscle, so removing its signal weakens, not strengthens, contractions. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the logic of a positive feedback loop to predict how interrupting its effector step alters the outcome, rather than recalling a stated fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '1658e9aa-8b48-4876-8e63-9d88b18281c6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '1658e9aa-8b48-4876-8e63-9d88b18281c6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('1658e9aa-8b48-4876-8e63-9d88b18281c6', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses positive feedback with homeostatic negative feedback'),
  ('1658e9aa-8b48-4876-8e63-9d88b18281c6', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'overweights a real but secondary contributor (prostaglandins)'),
  ('1658e9aa-8b48-4876-8e63-9d88b18281c6', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'treats oxytocin as uterine inhibitor like progesterone');

-- [Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During gestation, oxygen is delivered to the fetus only by diffusion across the placenta, where fetal blood is separated from maternal blood by a thin barrier and never mixes with it. A researcher measures the P50 (the partial pressure of O2 at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated) for blood drawn from a pregnant patient and from her fetus. For a net movement of oxygen from the maternal blood into the fetal blood to occur across the placenta, which relationship between the two hemoglobins must hold?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Fetal hemoglobin has a higher oxygen affinity than maternal hemoglobin, so its dissociation curve is shifted to the left and its P50 is lower"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Fetal hemoglobin has a lower oxygen affinity than maternal hemoglobin, so its dissociation curve is shifted to the right and its P50 is lower"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Fetal hemoglobin has the same oxygen affinity as maternal hemoglobin, so the two dissociation curves overlap and their P50 values are equal"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fetal hemoglobin has a higher oxygen affinity than maternal hemoglobin, so its dissociation curve is shifted to the right and its P50 is higher"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' Oxygen crosses the placenta only by passive diffusion down its partial-pressure gradient, and hemoglobin''s oxygen affinity is described by its dissociation curve: a curve shifted to the left reflects higher affinity and a lower P50 (hemoglobin reaches 50% saturation at a lower partial pressure of O2), while a curve shifted to the right reflects lower affinity and a higher P50 (OpenStax Biology 2e, Ch. 39.4). For oxygen to move from maternal to fetal blood, fetal hemoglobin must pull oxygen off maternal hemoglobin, which requires that fetal hemoglobin bind oxygen more avidly. (Choice A) is correct: fetal hemoglobin with a higher affinity has a left-shifted curve and a lower P50, so at the partial pressures found in the placenta it stays more saturated and draws oxygen from the lower-affinity maternal hemoglobin, producing net transfer into the fetus. (Choice B) is incorrect: a lower affinity would indeed shift the curve to the right, but a right shift raises P50 rather than lowering it, so this option pairs the correct affinity direction for maternal transfer failure with a contradictory curve/P50 description, and a lower-affinity fetal hemoglobin could not extract oxygen from maternal blood. (Choice C) is incorrect: if the two curves were identical there would be no affinity difference to drive net transfer at equal partial pressures, so oxygen would not preferentially move toward the fetus. (Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly states that fetal hemoglobin has higher affinity, but higher affinity corresponds to a left-shifted curve and a lower P50, not a right shift and higher P50, so the stated affinity and the curve/P50 description are inconsistent. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship among oxygen affinity, the direction of the dissociation-curve shift, and P50 to a novel gradient-driven diffusion scenario, rather than recalling a single fact.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ad0d2606-efea-4935-a077-f6627b4b4988';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ad0d2606-efea-4935-a077-f6627b4b4988';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ad0d2606-efea-4935-a077-f6627b4b4988', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflates affinity direction with P50/curve-shift direction'),
  ('ad0d2606-efea-4935-a077-f6627b4b4988', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes equal affinities suffice for directional diffusion'),
  ('ad0d2606-efea-4935-a077-f6627b4b4988', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'correct affinity but reversed curve-shift/P50 relationship');

-- [Placental Functions] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A perinatal physiologist studies how amino acids reach the fetus across the placenta. She measures the free amino acid concentration in blood on both sides of the placental barrier and finds it consistently about three times higher in the fetal circulation than in maternal blood. She then perfuses the placenta with a metabolic inhibitor that depletes cellular ATP. Net amino acid transfer to the fetus stops, while transfer of oxygen and of glucose continues unchanged. Which transport mechanism is responsible for delivering amino acids to the fetus?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Simple diffusion"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Facilitated diffusion"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Active transport"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Osmosis"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' The placenta takes over the fetus''s nutrient requirements, with the parent''s blood passing solutes across the placental barrier into the fetal circulation. Two observations must be reconciled: the fetal side already holds a higher amino acid concentration than the maternal side, yet transfer to the fetus continues, and that transfer stops the moment ATP is depleted. (Choice A) is incorrect: simple diffusion moves solutes only from high to low concentration and requires no ATP, so it cannot explain net delivery toward the already higher-concentration fetal side nor its dependence on ATP; this is why oxygen, which is more concentrated on the maternal side, keeps crossing when ATP is depleted. (Choice B) is incorrect: facilitated diffusion is carrier-mediated but still passive, so like the glucose transfer that continues after ATP depletion, it moves solutes down a gradient and cannot account for movement toward the higher-concentration side. (Choice C) is correct: only active transport couples solute movement to ATP hydrolysis, allowing amino acids to be moved toward the more concentrated fetal side; this alone explains both the maintained delivery against the concentration difference and the arrest of transfer when ATP is depleted. (Choice D) is incorrect: osmosis is the diffusion of water, not amino acids, across a membrane down its water potential gradient, and it neither transports solutes directly nor consumes ATP. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept that a transport process is active only if it both moves solute toward higher concentration and depends on ATP, inferring the mechanism from the concentration and inhibitor data rather than recalling a labeled fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '01c30f8f-fc0c-479a-9159-9c5d9b05fa9c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '01c30f8f-fc0c-479a-9159-9c5d9b05fa9c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('01c30f8f-fc0c-479a-9159-9c5d9b05fa9c', 'A', 'misconception', 'passive-route-for-all-solutes'),
  ('01c30f8f-fc0c-479a-9159-9c5d9b05fa9c', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'carrier-mediated-but-passive'),
  ('01c30f8f-fc0c-479a-9159-9c5d9b05fa9c', 'D', 'misconception', 'osmosis-drags-solute');

-- [Blastulation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A developmental biologist tracks a sea urchin embryo from the fertilized egg through the early blastula stage. She records the number of cells and the average diameter of individual cells (blastomeres) at the zygote, 8-cell, and early blastula stages. Over this interval, the cell count rises from 1 to several dozen. Based on how the early cleavage divisions partition the embryo, what should she expect for the total volume of the embryo and the size of individual blastomeres across these stages?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The total volume of the embryo stays roughly the same as the original zygote, while individual blastomeres become progressively smaller."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The total volume of the embryo increases in step with cell number, while individual blastomeres remain about the same size as the zygote."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The total volume of the embryo increases modestly as the internal cavity fills with fluid, while individual blastomeres grow slightly between divisions."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The total volume of the embryo decreases sharply as material is shed each division, while individual blastomeres shrink faster than their number rises."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' During cleavage, the fertilized egg is divided by a rapid series of mitotic divisions into smaller and smaller cells called blastomeres. The defining feature that distinguishes cleavage from ordinary cell division is that it partitions the pre-existing cytoplasm of the zygote without any net cell growth between divisions. (Choice A) is correct: the textbook states that in cleavage "the egg is subdivided by successive cleavages into smaller and smaller cells, with no actual cell growth," and that "the 8-cell stage and the blastula are about the same size as the original zygote"; because the same amount of material is repeatedly split among more cells, total embryo volume stays roughly constant while each blastomere gets smaller. (Choice B) is incorrect: it treats cleavage like typical growth, in which cells enlarge to full size before dividing and total mass increases; but cleavage divisions add no new cytoplasm, so total volume does not scale with cell number and the blastomeres cannot stay zygote-sized. (Choice C) is incorrect: although the blastula does develop a fluid-filled cavity, the embryo remains about the same overall size as the zygote, and blastomeres do not grow between the rapid cleavage divisions. (Choice D) is incorrect: cleavage redistributes the existing cytoplasm rather than discarding it, so the total volume is conserved rather than lost, and the increase in cell number is exactly what compensates for the shrinking size of each blastomere. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that cleavage subdivides fixed cytoplasm without growth to predict how total embryo volume and individual cell size change as cell number rises, rather than recalling a definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2802fe19-606e-4001-b2e3-13446caccdbc';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2802fe19-606e-4001-b2e3-13446caccdbc';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2802fe19-606e-4001-b2e3-13446caccdbc', 'B', 'misconception', 'cleavage-treated-as-ordinary-growth'),
  ('2802fe19-606e-4001-b2e3-13446caccdbc', 'C', 'misconception', 'blastocoel-fluid-enlarges-embryo'),
  ('2802fe19-606e-4001-b2e3-13446caccdbc', 'D', 'misconception', 'subdivision-loses-material');

-- [Gastrulation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studies a mouse embryo carrying a mutation that selectively disrupts the differentiation of a single primary germ layer during gastrulation. As development proceeds, the embryo fails to form normal skeletal cartilage, cardiac muscle, and kidney tissue, while its gut lining, respiratory epithelium, skin, and brain develop normally. The disrupted germ layer is the:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "mesoderm"}, {"label": "B", "text": "endoderm"}, {"label": "C", "text": "ectoderm"}, {"label": "D", "text": "trophoblast"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' During gastrulation, the three primary germ layers are established, and each is programmed to give rise to specific tissues: endoderm forms the lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts, ectoderm forms the outer body surface and the central nervous system, and mesoderm forms muscle, connective tissues such as skeleton and blood, and most visceral organs such as the kidneys. To answer, you must recognize that the affected structures (skeletal cartilage, cardiac muscle, kidney) all share one germ-layer origin while the spared structures (gut lining, respiratory epithelium, skin, brain) come from the other two layers. (Choice A) is correct: the mesoderm gives rise to all specialized muscle tissues (including cardiac muscle), connective tissues such as the skeleton, and most visceral organs including the kidneys, so a defect confined to mesoderm would impair exactly the skeletal, cardiac, and renal structures described while sparing endoderm- and ectoderm-derived tissues. (Choice B) is incorrect: the endoderm gives rise to the lining of the digestive tract and the respiratory epithelium, both of which develop normally in this embryo, so endoderm is not the disrupted layer. (Choice C) is incorrect: the ectoderm gives rise to the outer covering of the body (skin) and the central nervous system (brain), both of which are spared here, so ectoderm is not the disrupted layer. (Choice D) is incorrect: the trophoblast is an extraembryonic tissue that contributes to the placenta, not one of the three primary germ layers that generate the embryo''s tissues, so it does not account for the loss of skeleton, heart muscle, and kidney. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply knowledge of germ-layer derivatives to a novel loss-of-function pattern and infer which single germ layer accounts for all the affected structures.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '4fa40366-efde-49ae-820f-a201633a35db';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '4fa40366-efde-49ae-820f-a201633a35db';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('4fa40366-efde-49ae-820f-a201633a35db', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses internal/visceral organs with endoderm because they are deep in the body'),
  ('4fa40366-efde-49ae-820f-a201633a35db', 'C', 'misconception', 'mis-assigns connective/muscle tissue to the outer ectoderm layer'),
  ('4fa40366-efde-49ae-820f-a201633a35db', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'extraembryonic tissue mistaken for a primary germ layer');

-- [Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher fertilizes a mammalian egg in culture and measures the embryo at two time points: as a single-celled zygote, and again about three days later, after several rounds of division have produced a solid ball of many cells. She finds that the total volume of the multicellular embryo is nearly identical to that of the original zygote. Which statement best explains this observation?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The divisions partition the zygote into smaller cells without a growth phase between them, so cell number rises while total volume stays nearly constant."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Each daughter cell grows back to the zygote''s original size before the next division, so total volume increases in proportion to cell number."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The genome is not replicated before each division, so the cells share the original material and no net volume is added."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The dividing cells absorb nutrients from the surrounding medium, so total mass rises but the cells become denser and occupy the same volume."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' Animal development begins with cleavage, a rapid series of mitotic divisions of the zygote. Cleavage differs from ordinary somatic cell division because the egg is subdivided into smaller and smaller cells (blastomeres) with no actual cell growth between divisions, so the embryo''s total size stays roughly the same as the original zygote even as cell number climbs. (Choice A) is correct: because cleavage divisions lack the growth phase that lets a cell restore its size before dividing again, the fixed material of the zygote is simply partitioned into more and more, progressively smaller cells, keeping total volume nearly constant. (Choice B) is incorrect: if each daughter cell grew back to the zygote''s size before dividing, total volume would double with every round and the multicellular embryo would be far larger than the zygote — this is how typical proliferating somatic tissue grows, not how cleavage works. (Choice C) is incorrect: DNA is faithfully replicated before each cleavage division, so every blastomere receives a complete diploid genome; the constant volume comes from the absence of cytoplasmic growth, not from skipping replication. (Choice D) is incorrect: the pre-implantation embryo does not add net mass by taking up nutrients during cleavage — the near-constant volume reflects redistribution of the zygote''s existing material into smaller cells, not increased density. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining feature of cleavage — division without intervening cell growth — to interpret why the embryo''s total volume remains unchanged as cell number increases.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8d111c34-3381-4487-9807-4b650ad49443';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '8d111c34-3381-4487-9807-4b650ad49443';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('8d111c34-3381-4487-9807-4b650ad49443', 'B', 'misconception', 'treats cleavage like ordinary growth-coupled mitosis'),
  ('8d111c34-3381-4487-9807-4b650ad49443', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes genome is diluted/unreplicated across divisions'),
  ('8d111c34-3381-4487-9807-4b650ad49443', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes early embryo grows by nutrient uptake');

-- [Gestation & Birth] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A patient in active labor is having regular, intensifying uterine contractions. To slow preterm labor, a clinician administers a drug that blocks oxytocin receptors on uterine smooth muscle. Assuming the drug fully occupies these receptors, which outcome best predicts the effect on the labor process, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Contractions weaken and the escalating cycle stalls, because the loop driving labor amplifies its own output and depends on oxytocin acting at the uterus"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Contractions continue unchanged, because prostaglandins from the placenta independently sustain the full force of labor"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Contractions intensify further, because blocking the receptors removes the inhibitory signal that normally limits uterine contraction"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Contractions stop and then restart at a fixed rhythm, because a stable set point resets uterine activity to baseline"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' Labor (parturition) is driven by a positive feedback loop: cervical stretching triggers the posterior pituitary to release oxytocin, oxytocin binds receptors on uterine smooth muscle to cause contraction, and the resulting contraction stretches the cervix further, driving still more oxytocin release. Unlike negative feedback, this self-amplifying cycle escalates until the stimulus (the fetus) is expelled. Because oxytocin acting at its uterine receptors is a required link in this cycle, blocking those receptors interrupts the loop. (Choice A) is correct: with the receptors occupied, oxytocin can no longer drive contraction, so the amplifying cycle loses its output step and the escalation stalls—this is why oxytocin-receptor blockade is used to slow labor. (Choice B) is incorrect: although the placenta does release prostaglandins that augment contractions, they act to increase contractions alongside oxytocin rather than independently powering full labor, so removing oxytocin''s action still weakens the process. (Choice C) is incorrect: it treats oxytocin as an inhibitory signal, but oxytocin stimulates contraction, so blocking its receptor cannot intensify labor—this reverses the hormone''s true role. (Choice D) is incorrect: it describes a negative-feedback set point returning the system to baseline, but parturition is governed by positive feedback with no such stabilizing set point. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the logic of a positive feedback loop—identifying oxytocin''s uterine action as the amplifying step—to predict how a receptor blocker interrupts labor.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'dd1fcc4c-a667-4156-a861-a5bd6de4795b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'dd1fcc4c-a667-4156-a861-a5bd6de4795b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('dd1fcc4c-a667-4156-a861-a5bd6de4795b', 'B', 'partial_truth', 'real-mechanism-wrong-magnitude'),
  ('dd1fcc4c-a667-4156-a861-a5bd6de4795b', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'hormone-role-inverted'),
  ('dd1fcc4c-a667-4156-a861-a5bd6de4795b', 'D', 'misconception', 'positive-mistaken-for-negative-feedback');

-- [Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher labels one population of cells within a human blastocyst with a fluorescent marker just before the blastocyst reaches the uterus. Several days later, the marker is found only in the cells that have burrowed into the endometrium and are giving rise to the fetal portion of the placenta; no marker appears in the tissue that will become the embryo itself. Which cell population did the researcher most likely label?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The trophoblast, the outer layer of cells of the blastocyst"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The inner cell mass, the inner cluster of cells of the blastocyst"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The blastomeres, the cells produced by cleavage of the zygote"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The endometrial cells, the cells that line the wall of the uterus"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' In mammals the blastula reorganizes into a blastocyst whose cells separate into two populations: an inner cell mass and a surrounding outer layer. OpenStax Biology 2e states that in the blastocyst "the cells in the blastula arrange themselves in two layers: the inner cell mass, and an outer layer called the trophoblast," that "the trophoblast will contribute to the placenta and nourish the embryo," and that "the outer layers of the zygote (blastocyst) grow into the endometrium by digesting the endometrial cells" during implantation. Solving the item requires mapping two functional clues in the stem—burrowing into the endometrium and forming the fetal placenta—onto the correct cell population. (Choice A) is correct: the trophoblast is the outer layer of the blastocyst, and it is the tissue that invades the endometrium during implantation and contributes to the fetal portion of the placenta, exactly the behavior the marker traced. (Choice B) is incorrect: the inner cell mass (embryoblast) is the cluster of embryonic stem cells that differentiates into the tissues of the embryo itself, so it—not the labeled population—corresponds to the tissue the stem says was unmarked. (Choice C) is incorrect: blastomeres are the cells produced by cleavage before the blastocyst forms, an earlier and undifferentiated stage; by the time inner-versus-outer fates are set, labeling "the blastomeres" no longer identifies a single placenta-forming population. (Choice D) is incorrect: endometrial cells are maternal uterine-lining cells that the invading blastocyst digests, not blastocyst cells the researcher could label, and the fetal part of the placenta arises from embryonic tissue rather than from the mother''s endometrium. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the functional roles of the blastocyst''s two cell layers to a labeling experiment and infer which population''s behavior matches the observed pattern.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'af2df971-1fbd-4d7b-b5ed-7264ad9ced27';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'af2df971-1fbd-4d7b-b5ed-7264ad9ced27';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('af2df971-1fbd-4d7b-b5ed-7264ad9ced27', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swapping the fates of the inner cell mass and trophoblast'),
  ('af2df971-1fbd-4d7b-b5ed-7264ad9ced27', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'naming an earlier developmental stage''s cells'),
  ('af2df971-1fbd-4d7b-b5ed-7264ad9ced27', 'D', 'misconception', 'believing the maternal endometrium forms the fetal placenta');

-- [Cell-Cell Communication] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A developmental biologist studies cells that secrete a diffusible signaling molecule. Under normal conditions, the molecule triggers a fast, strong response that is confined to the cells lying immediately next to the source, and the response fades quickly. When the biologist applies a drug that blocks the extracellular enzymes that break down the molecule, the response instead spreads across a much wider region of tissue, activating cells that were previously never reached. Which mode of chemical signaling is the biologist most likely observing?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Endocrine signaling"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Paracrine signaling"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Autocrine signaling"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Direct signaling across gap junctions"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Mechanisms of Development.'' The four modes of chemical signaling differ mainly in the distance a ligand travels, but they also differ in how the response is spatially restricted: paracrine ligands diffuse locally through the extracellular matrix and are normally degraded quickly by enzymes so that the response stays confined to nearby cells and is fast and short-lived. The key inference here is that blocking those degrading enzymes lets the ligand persist and diffuse farther, which is exactly what expands the affected region — a behavior that identifies the local, diffusion-limited mode rather than one defined by transport or physical connections. (Choice B) is correct: paracrine signaling acts locally between nearby cells, and the text notes that "paracrine ligand molecules are normally quickly degraded by enzymes," which keeps the response localized; removing that degradation would let the ligand spread and recruit cells farther away, matching the observation. (Choice A) is incorrect: endocrine ligands are hormones carried "the large distances between endocrine cells and their target cells via the bloodstream," so the reach of an endocrine signal is set by circulation, not by local extracellular degradation, and would not expand simply because a degrading enzyme was inhibited. (Choice C) is incorrect: autocrine signals are received by "the signaling cell" itself or similar cells, so the defining feature is that the source and target are the same cell type, not a diffusion range that widens when degradation is blocked. (Choice D) is incorrect: gap-junction signaling moves small molecules "directly between adjacent cells" through channels rather than by diffusion through the extracellular space, so an extracellular enzyme inhibitor would not extend its reach across the tissue. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the property that rapid enzymatic degradation confines a diffusible ligand''s range to infer which signaling mode is affected when that degradation is blocked.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '950b5ec7-15b7-4df4-ac6a-2b2814831f15';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '950b5ec7-15b7-4df4-ac6a-2b2814831f15';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('950b5ec7-15b7-4df4-ac6a-2b2814831f15', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'confusing_secreted_signal_with_hormone'),
  ('950b5ec7-15b7-4df4-ac6a-2b2814831f15', 'C', 'misconception', 'conflating_local_action_with_self_signaling'),
  ('950b5ec7-15b7-4df4-ac6a-2b2814831f15', 'D', 'misconception', 'assuming_spread_to_neighbors_means_direct_coupling');

-- [Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a human blastocyst that has reached the uterus but not yet implanted, a mutation specifically disables the outer cell layer''s ability to digest and grow into endometrial tissue. Assuming all inner cells are unaffected, which outcome is best predicted?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Implantation into the uterine wall fails, because the outer cell layer is the tissue that normally embeds the conceptus and later contributes to the placenta"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Formation of the three primary germ layers fails, because the outer cell layer is the source of the cells that later gastrulate into the embryo"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The fluid-filled central cavity fails to form, because expansion of the outer cell layer is what hollows out the interior of the blastocyst"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cleavage halts before the blastula stage, because the outer cell layer is what drives the rapid early rounds of cell division"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' In the mammalian blastocyst, the cells arrange into two populations with a division of labor: an inner cell mass that becomes the embryo proper and an outer layer, the trophoblast, that mediates implantation and contributes to the placenta. The text states that "the outer layers of the zygote (blastocyst) grow into the endometrium by digesting the endometrial cells" during implantation, and that "the inner cell mass... will go on to form the embryo," while "the trophoblast will contribute to the placenta and nourish the embryo." (Choice A) is correct: the described defect is in the outer layer''s ability to invade the endometrium, and that outer trophoblast layer is precisely the tissue that embeds the conceptus into the uterine wall and later builds the placenta, so disabling it prevents implantation. (Choice B) is incorrect: the cells that gastrulate to form the endoderm, mesoderm, and ectoderm arise from the inner cell mass, not the outer trophoblast, so germ-layer formation is not the function lost with an outer-layer defect. (Choice C) is incorrect: the fluid-filled cavity (blastocoel) forms as the ball of blastomeres rearranges during blastula formation, a process that precedes and does not depend on the trophoblast''s endometrium-digesting activity. (Choice D) is incorrect: cleavage is the series of rapid divisions that produce the blastula before the trophoblast is even distinguished as a separate layer, so the outer layer does not drive cleavage and blastula formation would not be blocked. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the division of labor between the trophoblast and inner cell mass to a novel loss-of-function scenario and predict which specific developmental event is compromised.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '281356f0-e7f4-4fbd-8696-33f6b7d23e99';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '281356f0-e7f4-4fbd-8696-33f6b7d23e99';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('281356f0-e7f4-4fbd-8696-33f6b7d23e99', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing trophoblast with inner cell mass as the embryo-forming layer'),
  ('281356f0-e7f4-4fbd-8696-33f6b7d23e99', 'C', 'misconception', 'attributing blastocoel cavity formation to the trophoblast''s invasive function'),
  ('281356f0-e7f4-4fbd-8696-33f6b7d23e99', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'assigning cleavage-stage divisions to a later-differentiated layer');

-- [Placental Functions] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'By the middle of a normal human pregnancy, an obstetrician notes that the patient''s corpus luteum has fully degenerated, yet progesterone levels remain high and the uterine lining continues to be maintained, so the pregnancy proceeds without complication. Which structure is now responsible for producing the progesterone that sustains the endometrium?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The placenta, which has taken over secretion of progesterone as the corpus luteum degenerates"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The corpus luteum, which continues to secrete progesterone in response to placental hormone signals"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The endometrium, which begins secreting its own progesterone once the embryo has implanted"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The fetal adrenal glands, which supply the progesterone needed to maintain the uterine lining"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' Early in gestation, human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) from the developing embryo keeps the corpus luteum active so that its progesterone maintains the endometrium; as pregnancy advances, the placenta matures and assumes the endocrine functions of the pregnancy. The textbook states that by the second trimester "the placenta has taken over the functions of nutrition and waste and the production of estrogen and progesterone from the corpus luteum, which has degenerated," and that the placenta continues functioning through delivery. (Choice A) is correct: once the corpus luteum degenerates, the placenta itself produces the progesterone (and estrogen) that maintains the uterine lining and sustains the pregnancy, which is why the pregnancy proceeds normally despite loss of the corpus luteum. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the actual handoff—early on, embryonic hCG does sustain the corpus luteum, but the scenario specifies the corpus luteum has fully degenerated, so it can no longer secrete progesterone regardless of any signal. (Choice C) is incorrect: the endometrium is the tissue being maintained by progesterone; it responds to progesterone and provides early diffusion-based nutrient and waste exchange, but it does not itself become the source of the progesterone that sustains the pregnancy. (Choice D) is incorrect: the fetal adrenal glands do contribute precursors used in placental estrogen synthesis late in pregnancy, but they are not the source of the progesterone that maintains the endometrium—that role belongs to the placenta. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of the endocrine handoff from corpus luteum to placenta to explain how progesterone and the uterine lining are maintained after the corpus luteum is gone.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '9ab264e3-c534-47ac-b16e-f518c54ca5df';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9ab264e3-c534-47ac-b16e-f518c54ca5df';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9ab264e3-c534-47ac-b16e-f518c54ca5df', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'confusing the early hCG/corpus-luteum phase with the later placental phase'),
  ('9ab264e3-c534-47ac-b16e-f518c54ca5df', 'C', 'misconception', 'treating the target tissue (endometrium) as the hormone source'),
  ('9ab264e3-c534-47ac-b16e-f518c54ca5df', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'fetal adrenal supplies estrogen precursors, not maintenance progesterone');

-- [Foramen Ovale & Ductus Arteriosus] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During neurulation in an amphibian embryo, an experimenter uses a drug to block the growth-factor signals that normally act on the cells at the outer edge of the ectoderm. Compared with an untreated embryo, what is the most likely fate of this treated ectoderm?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A larger-than-normal fraction of the ectoderm becomes neural tissue."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A larger-than-normal fraction of the ectoderm becomes epidermis."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The ectoderm fails to form neural tissue and stays undifferentiated."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The ectoderm forms neural tissue and epidermis in the usual proportion."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Embryogenesis & Development,'' testing ectodermal cell-fate specification during neurulation/organogenesis. During organogenesis the ectoderm can give rise to two derivatives, the nervous system and the epidermis, and which cells take which path is set by growth-factor signaling: the text explains that these growth factors ''signal some cells at the edge of the ectoderm to become epidermis cells,'' while ''the remaining cells in the center form the neural plate'' that rolls into the neural tube. The key inference is that neural fate is the default state of ectoderm; the signal is what diverts edge cells away from that default and toward epidermis. (Choice A) is correct: the textbook states outright that ''if the signaling by growth factors were disrupted, then the entire ectoderm would differentiate into neural tissue,'' so removing the signal that pushes edge cells toward epidermis leaves more of the ectoderm following its default neural path. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the role of the signal—the growth factor induces epidermis, not neural tissue, so blocking it yields less epidermis, not more. (Choice C) is incorrect: ectoderm does not require this signal to become neural; neural differentiation is the default fate that proceeds in the signal''s absence, so the tissue does not remain undifferentiated. (Choice D) is incorrect: this edge signal is precisely what partitions ectoderm into neural versus epidermal regions, so removing it shifts the proportions rather than leaving them unchanged. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept that ectoderm has a default neural fate overridden by an inductive signal, then predict how removing that signal redistributes the two ectodermal derivatives.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '0f8fa0dd-360d-4c05-8be3-9c82eb35c107';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0f8fa0dd-360d-4c05-8be3-9c82eb35c107';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0f8fa0dd-360d-4c05-8be3-9c82eb35c107', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps which fate the inductive growth-factor signal promotes (epidermis vs. neural)'),
  ('0f8fa0dd-360d-4c05-8be3-9c82eb35c107', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats the growth-factor signal as required for neural induction rather than for diverting cells to epidermis'),
  ('0f8fa0dd-360d-4c05-8be3-9c82eb35c107', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes the edge signal has no patterning role, so ectodermal proportions stay fixed');

-- [Foramen Ovale & Ductus Arteriosus] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A teratogen disrupts a signaling pathway required for the middle germ layer to form and differentiate normally in a triploblastic embryo. On follow-up imaging, the child''s cardiac muscle is found to be malformed. Which additional structure is MOST likely to have developed abnormally as a result of the same disruption?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The epidermis covering the outer body surface"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The neurons that make up the spinal cord"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The epithelial lining of the small intestine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The kidneys of the urinary system"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question about embryogenesis and the major structures arising out of the primary germ layers. During gastrulation, the three primary germ layers are established, and each is programmed to give rise to a specific set of tissues and organs; the middle germ layer of a triploblast is the mesoderm. Because cardiac muscle is a mesodermal derivative, a defect that impairs mesoderm formation should also disrupt other mesodermal structures, so the task is to identify which listed structure shares that mesodermal origin. The textbook states that the mesoderm "gives rise to all specialized muscle tissues (including the cardiac tissues... ), connective tissues such as the skeleton and blood cells, and most other visceral organs such as the kidneys and the spleen." (Choice A) is incorrect: the outer epithelial covering of the body surface (epidermis) develops from ectoderm, not mesoderm, so it would not share the same origin as cardiac muscle. (Choice B) is incorrect: the central nervous system, including the neurons of the spinal cord, arises from ectoderm via neurulation; assuming the CNS comes from the middle layer is a common error. (Choice C) is incorrect: the lining of the digestive tract, including the epithelium of the small intestine, is an endoderm derivative, not mesoderm. (Choice D) is correct: the kidneys arise from mesoderm, the same germ layer that forms cardiac muscle, so a disruption of mesoderm would be expected to affect both. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the germ-layer origins of adult structures to predict which organ shares a developmental lineage with cardiac muscle.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'e61b585f-b0de-4c85-9e57-4f895639878e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e61b585f-b0de-4c85-9e57-4f895639878e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e61b585f-b0de-4c85-9e57-4f895639878e', 'A', 'misconception', 'wrong_germ_layer_ectoderm'),
  ('e61b585f-b0de-4c85-9e57-4f895639878e', 'B', 'misconception', 'mesoderm_gives_rise_to_CNS'),
  ('e61b585f-b0de-4c85-9e57-4f895639878e', 'C', 'misconception', 'wrong_germ_layer_endoderm');

-- [Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In an early pregnancy, the corpus luteum secretes the progesterone that maintains the uterine endometrium. By the second trimester, the corpus luteum has degenerated, yet the endometrium continues to be maintained and the pregnancy proceeds normally. Which change best accounts for the pregnancy continuing after the corpus luteum is gone?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The placenta assumes production of the progesterone that maintains the endometrium."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The corpus luteum remains active and secretes progesterone throughout the entire pregnancy."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The endometrium itself begins synthesizing enough progesterone to sustain its own maintenance."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Chorionic gonadotropin acts directly on the endometrium to keep it intact without progesterone."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' During the reproductive sequence, the endometrium must be kept intact by progesterone for the pregnancy to continue, and the source of that progesterone shifts as gestation progresses. Early on, the chorion releases chorionic gonadotropin that keeps the corpus luteum active so it produces progesterone; later, the placenta takes over that endocrine role. (Choice A) is correct: the textbook states that during the second trimester the placenta has taken over "the production of estrogen and progesterone from the corpus luteum, which has degenerated," so placental progesterone maintains the endometrium once the corpus luteum is gone. (Choice B) is incorrect: the corpus luteum does not persist throughout pregnancy — the text explicitly notes it has degenerated by the second trimester, which is precisely why another progesterone source is required. (Choice C) is incorrect: the endometrium is the target tissue that progesterone maintains, not an endocrine gland that manufactures its own progesterone; its upkeep depends on hormone supplied by the corpus luteum and then the placenta. (Choice D) is incorrect: chorionic gonadotropin does not substitute for progesterone at the endometrium — it acts on the corpus luteum to keep it active and thereby "ensures adequate levels of progesterone that will maintain the endometrium," so the endometrium still depends on progesterone. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the shift in the progesterone source during gestation to explain why the pregnancy survives the loss of the corpus luteum, rather than recalling a single stated fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'e7ad984f-1e62-41ad-b32e-51732aabb014';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e7ad984f-1e62-41ad-b32e-51732aabb014';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e7ad984f-1e62-41ad-b32e-51732aabb014', 'B', 'misconception', 'corpus luteum persists all pregnancy'),
  ('e7ad984f-1e62-41ad-b32e-51732aabb014', 'C', 'misconception', 'target tissue confused with hormone source'),
  ('e7ad984f-1e62-41ad-b32e-51732aabb014', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'hCG acts directly on endometrium instead of corpus luteum');

-- [Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A physiologist studies a full-term fetus in utero and finds that its lungs are collapsed and filled with fluid, yet its arterial blood is well oxygenated. Moments after birth, the newborn takes its first breath and the umbilical cord is clamped. Which change best explains how the newborn now obtains oxygen compared with before birth?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Before birth, oxygen was loaded onto fetal blood at the placenta; after birth, oxygen is loaded at the newborn''s own lungs."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Before birth, the fetal lungs already oxygenated the blood; after birth, the placenta continues this role for several weeks."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Before birth, oxygen diffused directly across the fetal skin; after birth, the newborn''s lungs take over gas exchange."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Before birth, the maternal heart pumped oxygen-rich blood into the fetus; after birth, the fetal heart begins pumping for the first time."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' During gestation the fetal lungs are fluid-filled and non-functional, so gas exchange cannot occur there; instead the placenta serves as the fetal organ of exchange, with oxygen and nutrients passing from the parent''s blood to the fetal blood and waste moving in the opposite direction. OpenStax Biology 2e states that the placenta ''takes over the nutrient and waste requirements of the embryo and fetus, with the parent''s blood passing nutrients to the placenta and removing waste from it,'' and that during early development organs ''such as the lungs and liver, are not yet functioning.'' At birth, the newborn''s first breath inflates the lungs and clamping the cord ends placental exchange, so the lungs must take over oxygen loading. (Choice A) is correct: fetal blood is oxygenated at the placenta, and only after the first breath and cord clamping do the newborn''s own lungs become the site where oxygen is loaded onto the blood. (Choice B) is incorrect: the fetal lungs are fluid-filled and do not oxygenate blood before birth, and the placenta stops functioning once the cord is clamped rather than continuing for weeks. (Choice C) is incorrect: the human fetus does not use skin diffusion for gas exchange; oxygen is supplied across the placenta, not the fetal skin. (Choice D) is incorrect: the fetal heart pumps throughout gestation, and the parent''s blood does not pump directly into the fetus—maternal and fetal blood exchange gases across the placenta without the maternal heart driving fetal circulation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that fetal gas exchange occurs at the placenta while non-functional lungs wait to inflate, then predict how the oxygen source shifts once breathing begins and the cord is clamped.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '3117ba5a-c52c-4189-a1b8-3a82007c2e1a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3117ba5a-c52c-4189-a1b8-3a82007c2e1a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3117ba5a-c52c-4189-a1b8-3a82007c2e1a', 'B', 'misconception', 'believes fetal lungs are already functional in utero'),
  ('3117ba5a-c52c-4189-a1b8-3a82007c2e1a', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses mammalian placental exchange with amphibian/embryonic skin diffusion'),
  ('3117ba5a-c52c-4189-a1b8-3a82007c2e1a', 'D', 'misconception', 'believes maternal and fetal circulations are directly connected');

-- [Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In an in-vitro study of early mammalian development, researchers use a laser to selectively destroy the outer cell layer of a healthy blastocyst while leaving its inner cell mass fully intact. The manipulated blastocyst is then placed against a prepared uterine wall. Compared with an untreated blastocyst, which outcome is the most likely direct consequence of this manipulation?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Implantation fails and no placental tissue forms, so the blastocyst cannot embed in the endometrium."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Implantation proceeds, but the resulting embryo develops without a nervous system."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The blastocyst still embeds in the uterine wall, but it can no longer generate any embryonic stem cells."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Implantation occurs, yet the inner cell mass now differentiates into placental tissue rather than the embryo."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' In mammals, the blastula reorganizes into a blastocyst made of two distinct cell populations: an inner cell mass (embryoblast) that forms the embryo proper, and an outer layer called the trophoblast. The textbook states that the trophoblast "will contribute to the placenta and nourish the embryo," and that during implantation the "outer layers of the zygote (blastocyst) grow into the endometrium by digesting the endometrial cells." The laser in this experiment destroys precisely this outer layer. (Choice A) is correct: because the trophoblast is the layer that invades the endometrium to implant and later gives rise to the placenta, destroying it removes both the implantation machinery and the source of placental tissue. (Choice B) is incorrect: the nervous system arises later from ectoderm, one of the primary germ layers derived from the inner cell mass, which was left intact here, so nervous-system formation is not what the outer-layer ablation eliminates. (Choice C) is incorrect: embryonic stem cells reside in the inner cell mass, not the trophoblast, so leaving the inner cell mass intact preserves the stem-cell population rather than eliminating it. (Choice D) is incorrect: the inner cell mass is committed to forming the embryo and does not substitute for the missing trophoblast, so it does not convert into placental tissue when the outer layer is removed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the division of labor between the trophoblast and inner cell mass to predict the consequence of selectively removing one blastocyst layer.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b11f1ad3-088b-47c4-a4ac-4f71cb42032b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b11f1ad3-088b-47c4-a4ac-4f71cb42032b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b11f1ad3-088b-47c4-a4ac-4f71cb42032b', 'B', 'misconception', 'attributes an inner-cell-mass/germ-layer outcome to loss of the trophoblast'),
  ('b11f1ad3-088b-47c4-a4ac-4f71cb42032b', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps trophoblast and inner cell mass as the source of embryonic stem cells'),
  ('b11f1ad3-088b-47c4-a4ac-4f71cb42032b', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes the inner cell mass is plastic and can take over the placental role');

-- [Twins] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During gastrulation in a vertebrate embryo, a mutation prevents cells of the middle primary germ layer from differentiating normally, while the inner and outer germ layers are unaffected. Which structure is most likely to fail to develop as a direct result?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The central nervous system, including the brain and spinal cord"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The epithelial lining of the stomach and intestines"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The kidneys and the connective tissue of the skeleton"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The outer epithelial covering of the body surface"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' During gastrulation the embryo establishes three primary germ layers, and each layer is programmed to give rise to specific tissues and organs. The middle germ layer that forms between the inner and outer layers in triploblasts is the mesoderm, so a mutation disrupting the middle layer specifically compromises mesodermal derivatives. (Choice A) is incorrect: the central nervous system develops from the outer germ layer (ectoderm), which the text lists along with the outer epithelial covering of the body surface as ectodermal derivatives; confusing the CNS as a mesodermal structure is a classic error. (Choice B) is incorrect: the epithelial lining of the digestive tract, including the stomach and intestines, arises from the inner germ layer (endoderm); the mesoderm contributes the muscle of the gut wall, not its epithelial lining, so this lining would still form. (Choice C) is correct: the text states the mesoderm gives rise to specialized muscle, connective tissues such as the skeleton and blood cells, and most other visceral organs such as the kidneys, so disrupting the middle layer directly compromises the kidneys and skeletal connective tissue. (Choice D) is incorrect: the outer epithelial covering of the body surface is an ectodermal derivative from the outer germ layer, which is stated to be unaffected. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the germ-layer fate map to a novel ablation scenario, identifying the middle layer as mesoderm and predicting which structures lose their developmental source.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '17be4876-9e19-48bc-b777-c73413b10080';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '17be4876-9e19-48bc-b777-c73413b10080';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('17be4876-9e19-48bc-b777-c73413b10080', 'A', 'misconception', 'CNS misattributed to mesoderm rather than ectoderm'),
  ('17be4876-9e19-48bc-b777-c73413b10080', 'B', 'misconception', 'gut lining assigned to mesoderm because mesoderm forms gut muscle'),
  ('17be4876-9e19-48bc-b777-c73413b10080', 'D', 'misconception', 'body-surface epithelium confused as mesodermal derivative');

-- [Ectoderm Derivatives] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a vertebrate embryo during neurulation, growth factors normally signal the cells at the edge of the ectoderm to adopt an epidermal fate, while the central cells form the neural plate. A researcher engineers an embryo in which this growth-factor signaling is completely abolished across the entire ectoderm. Which outcome would the researcher most likely observe for the ectoderm?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The edge cells form the neural plate while the central cells become epidermis, reversing the normal arrangement."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The entire ectoderm differentiates into neural tissue rather than partitioning into neural and epidermal domains."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The entire ectoderm differentiates into epidermis, and no neural plate or neural tube forms at all."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The ectoderm partitions normally into a central neural plate and peripheral epidermis, since the pattern is signal-independent."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' The vertebrate ectoderm gives rise to both the epidermis and neural tissue, and which fate a given region adopts is decided during neurulation by growth-factor signaling: the textbook states that these signals push cells "at the edge of the ectoderm to become epidermis cells," while "the remaining cells in the center form the neural plate." The critical, counterintuitive point is what the growth-factor signal actually does — it diverts cells toward the epidermal fate, so the default fate of ectoderm in the absence of that signal is neural. (Choice B) is correct: the textbook explicitly states that "if the signaling by growth factors were disrupted, then the entire ectoderm would differentiate into neural tissue," because removing the signal removes the only cue that would have redirected the edge cells to epidermis. (Choice A) is incorrect: abolishing the signal does not swap the positions of the two fates; there is no residual signal to relocate the neural plate to the periphery, and the outcome is loss of the epidermal domain entirely, not a reversal. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the actual role of the signal — students often assume the growth factor promotes the neural fate, but the signal drives the epidermal fate, so its loss expands neural tissue, not epidermis. (Choice D) is incorrect: the neural-versus-epidermal partition is not signal-independent; it depends entirely on the graded growth-factor signal, so eliminating that signal collapses the normal pattern rather than preserving it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the specific role of growth-factor signaling in ectodermal fate choice to predict the outcome of a novel perturbation, rather than recall the normal fate map.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f8a331eb-4c06-43c9-be5b-9fdf6c78a2c1';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f8a331eb-4c06-43c9-be5b-9fdf6c78a2c1';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f8a331eb-4c06-43c9-be5b-9fdf6c78a2c1', 'A', 'misconception', 'spatial-reversal-of-fate-map'),
  ('f8a331eb-4c06-43c9-be5b-9fdf6c78a2c1', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'inverts-role-of-inducing-signal'),
  ('f8a331eb-4c06-43c9-be5b-9fdf6c78a2c1', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes-pattern-is-hardwired-signal-independent');

-- [Mesoderm Derivatives] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During gut development, a researcher uses a targeted signal to selectively block the mesodermal cells surrounding the endodermal gut tube from proliferating, while leaving the endoderm and any migrating ectodermal cells unaffected. In the resulting intestinal wall, which structure would be expected to fail to form normally?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The circular and longitudinal muscle coats of the intestinal wall"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The absorptive epithelium lining the intestinal lumen"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The neurons of the enteric nervous system"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The mucus-secreting goblet cells of the lumenal surface"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' The gut tube is a mixed-origin structure: the endoderm supplies the inner lining, the mesoderm supplies the muscle and connective-tissue coats around it, and ectodermal (neural crest) cells migrate in to build the enteric nervous system. Blocking only the mesoderm therefore knocks out whichever gut-wall structures the mesoderm normally builds, while sparing endodermal and ectodermal contributions. (Choice A) is correct: the text states that the mesoderm "gives rise to all specialized muscle tissues (including... the muscles of the intestines)" and to connective tissues, so eliminating the mesoderm removes the circular and longitudinal muscle coats of the intestinal wall. (Choice B) is incorrect: the absorptive epithelium is the lining of the digestive tract, which the endoderm generates; because the endoderm is left intact, this layer would still form. (Choice C) is incorrect: enteric neurons are nervous tissue, which derives from the ectoderm (neural crest), not the mesoderm, so they are spared when only mesoderm is blocked. (Choice D) is incorrect: goblet cells are part of the mucosal lining epithelium of the digestive tract, an endodermal derivative, so they too would still form despite the mesodermal block. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the assignment of gut-wall structures to their germ layers to predict which component fails when only the mesodermal contribution is removed.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '35d7c4f5-237b-4394-8201-d2394d55d4f4';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '35d7c4f5-237b-4394-8201-d2394d55d4f4';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('35d7c4f5-237b-4394-8201-d2394d55d4f4', 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong-germ-layer-assignment'),
  ('35d7c4f5-237b-4394-8201-d2394d55d4f4', 'C', 'misconception', 'wrong-germ-layer-assignment'),
  ('35d7c4f5-237b-4394-8201-d2394d55d4f4', 'D', 'misconception', 'wrong-germ-layer-assignment');

-- [Gastrulation] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During gastrulation in a triploblastic embryo, a researcher uses a permanent fluorescent label to mark all cells of one primary germ layer, then examines the fully developed animal to see which adult tissues carry the label. The labeled tissues include the skeletal muscles, the heart, the kidneys, and the red blood cells, but not the epidermis, the neurons, or the epithelial lining of the gut. Which germ layer did the researcher most likely label?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Ectoderm, because it produces both the muscles of the body wall and the outer epidermis"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Endoderm, because it produces the kidneys as well as the epithelial lining of the digestive tract"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Mesoderm, because it produces the skeletal muscle, heart, kidneys, and blood cells"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Ectoderm, because it produces both the neurons of the nervous system and the skeletal muscle"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' During gastrulation the three primary germ layers are established, and each is programmed to give rise to a specific set of adult tissues: the ectoderm forms the outer epidermis and the nervous system, the endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts, and the mesoderm forms muscle, connective tissue, blood, and most visceral organs. Identifying the labeled layer requires matching the observed pattern of derivatives to the layer that produces all of them. (Choice C) is correct: the mesoderm "gives rise to all specialized muscle tissues (including the cardiac tissues), connective tissues such as the skeleton and blood cells, and most other visceral organs such as the kidneys and the spleen" (OpenStax Biology 2e), which is exactly the labeled set—skeletal muscle, heart, kidneys, and red blood cells. (Choice A) is incorrect: although the muscles do derive from a single layer, that layer is the mesoderm, not the ectoderm; the ectoderm produces the epidermis and nervous system, both of which were explicitly unlabeled in this experiment. (Choice B) is incorrect: the endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the gut and respiratory tract, not the kidneys, and the gut lining was among the unlabeled tissues, so endoderm cannot be the labeled layer. (Choice D) is incorrect: the ectoderm does give rise to neurons, but the neurons were unlabeled, and skeletal muscle is a mesodermal derivative rather than an ectodermal one, so this pairing misassigns muscle to the wrong layer. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the known derivatives of each germ layer to an observed labeling pattern in order to deduce which layer was marked.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5', 'A', 'misconception', 'assigns muscle to ectoderm instead of mesoderm'),
  ('792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5', 'B', 'misconception', 'assigns visceral organ (kidney) to endoderm instead of mesoderm'),
  ('792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5', 'D', 'misconception', 'assigns skeletal muscle to ectoderm alongside its true neural derivative');

-- [Blastulation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher fertilizes a sea urchin egg and tracks the embryo as it progresses from the single-celled zygote to a hollow blastula. At each time point she records the number of cells and the total volume of the whole embryo. Over several hours the number of cells rises from 1 to more than 100, yet the total volume of the embryo remains nearly unchanged. Which conclusion about this period of development is best supported by these observations?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Cell division subdivides the fixed pool of egg cytoplasm without net growth, so each new cell is smaller than the cell it came from."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Cell division is accompanied by cytoplasmic growth, so each new cell is roughly the same size as the original zygote."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Cell division draws material from stored yolk, so total volume stays constant as yolk is steadily converted into new cells."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cell division is offset by programmed cell death, so total volume stays constant as new cells continually replace dying ones."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' Cleavage is the series of mitotic divisions that carries the zygote to the blastula stage, and it differs from ordinary somatic cell division in a key way: the egg is subdivided into smaller and smaller cells with no actual cell growth. Because no new cytoplasm is added, the whole embryo stays about the same size as the original zygote even as cell number climbs into the hundreds. (Choice A) is correct: with no growth phase between divisions, each round of cleavage splits the existing cytoplasm among more cells, so the blastomeres get progressively smaller while total volume holds constant — exactly the pattern the data show. (Choice B) is incorrect: this describes typical mitosis of a growing tissue, in which cells replenish their contents between divisions; if that occurred here, total volume would rise roughly in step with cell number, which contradicts the constant volume observed. (Choice C) is incorrect: yolk is a stored food reserve that nourishes the later embryo, but converting yolk into new cytoplasm would still add usable cell material and increase, not conserve, the embryo''s volume during cleavage. (Choice D) is incorrect: cleavage in this early period is characterized by rapid net increase in cell number, not by a balance of division and death; cell death cannot explain constant volume when the cell count is rising steeply. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining feature of cleavage — division without growth — to interpret data showing rising cell number at constant embryo volume.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '31802c86-0938-4162-a1d8-ffb6b92f8639';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '31802c86-0938-4162-a1d8-ffb6b92f8639';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('31802c86-0938-4162-a1d8-ffb6b92f8639', 'B', 'misconception', 'treats cleavage as ordinary growth-coupled mitosis'),
  ('31802c86-0938-4162-a1d8-ffb6b92f8639', 'C', 'misconception', 'invokes yolk conversion to explain constant volume'),
  ('31802c86-0938-4162-a1d8-ffb6b92f8639', 'D', 'misconception', 'attributes constant volume to apoptosis offsetting division');

-- [Foramen Ovale & Ductus Arteriosus] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher exposes an early triploblastic embryo to a teratogen that selectively blocks the development of a single primary germ layer. After exposure, the embryo fails to form functional cardiac muscle, kidneys, and the bony skeleton, while its skin epidermis and gut lining develop normally. Disruption of which germ layer best accounts for this specific pattern of defects?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Endoderm, the layer that produces the internal organs of the body"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Mesoderm, the layer that produces muscle, connective tissue, and most visceral organs"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ectoderm, the layer that produces the outer covering and internal structures"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Endoderm, the layer that produces the lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Embryogenesis & Development.'' During gastrulation, an embryo establishes three primary germ layers, and each is programmed to give rise to specific tissues: the ectoderm forms the epidermis and central nervous system, the endoderm forms the linings of the digestive and respiratory tracts, and the mesoderm forms all specialized muscle tissue, connective tissues such as the skeleton and blood, and most visceral organs. The task requires tracing several affected structures back to a single common origin rather than recalling one structure-to-layer pairing. (Choice B) is correct: cardiac muscle, the kidneys, and the bony skeleton all arise from mesoderm, which produces specialized muscle, connective tissue (including bone), and most visceral organs, so blocking mesoderm alone accounts for exactly this cluster of defects while sparing the epidermis and gut lining. (Choice A) is incorrect: although the kidneys are internal organs, endoderm does not give rise to visceral organs generally—it forms epithelial linings—so this describes the wrong layer, and the intact gut lining actually argues against endoderm damage. (Choice C) is incorrect: ectoderm forms the epidermis and nervous system, but the epidermis developed normally here, and ectoderm does not produce cardiac muscle, kidneys, or bone. (Choice D) is incorrect: endoderm does form the digestive and respiratory linings, but those structures were spared, and endoderm produces none of the muscle, renal, or skeletal tissues that were lost. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply germ-layer derivation rules to a novel injury pattern and identify the single layer whose loss explains all of the observed defects while sparing the unaffected tissues.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '719a55f6-3592-4f66-a8b7-4449c700e235';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '719a55f6-3592-4f66-a8b7-4449c700e235';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('719a55f6-3592-4f66-a8b7-4449c700e235', 'A', 'misconception', 'endoderm makes all internal/visceral organs'),
  ('719a55f6-3592-4f66-a8b7-4449c700e235', 'C', 'misconception', 'ectoderm as a catch-all outer and inner layer'),
  ('719a55f6-3592-4f66-a8b7-4449c700e235', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'correct endoderm derivatives that were spared in the scenario');

-- [Blastulation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher measures the overall diameter of a mammalian embryo at the single-celled zygote stage and again once cleavage has produced a blastula of over 100 cells. She finds that the two structures are approximately the same overall size, even though the blastula contains vastly more cells. Which statement best explains this observation?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The cells divide repeatedly without growing between divisions, so the original mass is partitioned into progressively smaller cells."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Each daughter cell grows back to the parent cell''s size before dividing again, so the embryo expands only after cleavage ends."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The cleavage divisions are meiotic, so each round halves the cells'' chromosome number rather than adding cytoplasm."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The dividing cells secrete their newly made cytoplasm into the central cavity, keeping the outer dimensions fixed."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' Cleavage is a series of rapid mitotic divisions that subdivide the zygote into smaller and smaller cells; the textbook states that during cleavage "the cells divide without an increase in mass" and that "the 8-cell stage and the blastula are about the same size as the original zygote." Because no cellular growth occurs between divisions, the fixed amount of cytoplasm from the zygote is simply repartitioned among an increasing number of ever-smaller blastomeres, leaving the overall dimensions of the embryo nearly unchanged. (Choice A) is correct: repeated division without intervening growth divides the zygote''s original mass among progressively smaller cells, so cell number rises while total size stays roughly constant. (Choice B) is incorrect: if each daughter cell regained the parent''s size before dividing, the embryo would grow rapidly with each round; the defining feature of cleavage is precisely the absence of such growth. (Choice C) is incorrect: cleavage divisions are mitotic, not meiotic, so each blastomere retains the full diploid chromosome number, and chromosome number is unrelated to why overall size stays constant. (Choice D) is incorrect: cells do not export cytoplasm into the blastocoel; the cavity fills with fluid, and the constant overall size reflects a lack of net cytoplasmic synthesis rather than secretion of cytoplasm. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that cleavage divides a fixed cytoplasmic mass without growth to explain why the blastula and zygote have nearly the same overall size.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c232dc0d-aca8-4604-b414-e0c478c0109b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c232dc0d-aca8-4604-b414-e0c478c0109b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c232dc0d-aca8-4604-b414-e0c478c0109b', 'B', 'misconception', 'development equals growth'),
  ('c232dc0d-aca8-4604-b414-e0c478c0109b', 'C', 'misconception', 'cleavage confused with meiosis'),
  ('c232dc0d-aca8-4604-b414-e0c478c0109b', 'D', 'misconception', 'misattributed mechanism for constant size');

-- [Gestation & Birth] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During labor, a clinician records that each uterine contraction is stronger than the one before it, and that the interval between contractions shortens as the cervix dilates further. The contractions cease only after the baby is delivered and the cervix is no longer being stretched. Which mechanism best explains this escalating, self-terminating pattern?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Each contraction stretches the cervix further, and this stretch drives more oxytocin release, so the response amplifies the same stimulus that triggered it."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Rising oxytocin is detected and opposed so that contraction strength is held near a fixed set point, so the response counteracts the stimulus that triggered it."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Oxytocin acts directly on uterine muscle with no sensory signal returning to the hypothalamus, so contraction strength stays fixed at the level set by the initial hormone release."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Placental prostaglandins alone sustain the contractions, so circulating oxytocin falls steadily as labor advances toward delivery."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' Parturition is driven by a positive feedback loop: cervical stretch sends nerve impulses to the hypothalamus, which triggers oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary; oxytocin makes the uterine smooth muscle contract, which pushes the fetus against the cervix and stretches it still further. Because each contraction intensifies the very signal that caused it, contractions escalate rather than settling at a set point, and the loop is only broken when delivery removes the cervical stretch. (Choice A) is correct: contraction causes further cervical stretch, which drives still more oxytocin, so the output reinforces its own trigger — the defining feature of a positive feedback loop, which is why the pattern escalates and stops only when the stretch (the stimulus) is removed at birth. (Choice B) is incorrect: opposing a rising signal to hold an output near a set point describes negative feedback, the predominant homeostatic mechanism (as in insulin lowering blood glucose); it would damp the contractions rather than let them intensify, which contradicts the escalating pattern observed. (Choice C) is incorrect: an open loop with no sensory return would give a fixed contraction strength set by the initial dose, but the recorded data show contractions growing stronger over time, which requires an amplifying feedback signal, not a fixed one. (Choice D) is incorrect: prostaglandins augment the contractions but do not replace oxytocin, and oxytocin actually rises rather than falls during labor; declining oxytocin cannot explain contractions that grow progressively more forceful. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of feedback loop structure to novel clinical data (escalating, stretch-dependent contractions) and infer that only self-amplifying positive feedback fits the observed pattern.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'e57dcb02-c0fa-4d06-9365-a67dd3ee9e3a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e57dcb02-c0fa-4d06-9365-a67dd3ee9e3a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e57dcb02-c0fa-4d06-9365-a67dd3ee9e3a', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses positive feedback with negative feedback (assumes homeostatic set-point control)'),
  ('e57dcb02-c0fa-4d06-9365-a67dd3ee9e3a', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats the pathway as an open loop with no sensory feedback'),
  ('e57dcb02-c0fa-4d06-9365-a67dd3ee9e3a', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'assumes oxytocin falls during labor and prostaglandins act alone');

-- [Twins] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In an early deuterostome embryo undergoing cleavage, a researcher uses a fine needle to separate one blastomere from the rest of the cell mass and lets both parts continue developing. Which outcome should the researcher expect?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The isolated blastomere develops into a complete embryo, and the remaining cells compensate to form a normal embryo."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The isolated blastomere and the remaining cells each develop while missing the structures the other portion would have formed."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The isolated blastomere develops normally, but the remaining cells cannot compensate and fail to form an embryo."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Both the isolated blastomere and the remaining cells arrest because the embryo has lost cells during cleavage."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' Cleavage is a series of rapid mitotic divisions of the zygote, and in deuterostomes it is indeterminate: the fate of each early blastomere is not yet fixed. The textbook states that in deuterostomes, cells are "not yet fully committed at this early stage," that removing a blastomere "does not result in the loss of embryonic structures," that separated blastomeres can even produce "twins (clones)," and that adjacent cells "are able to compensate for the missing cells" so the embryo is not damaged. (Choice A) is correct: because these early blastomeres are undetermined, an isolated one retains the potential to build a whole organism (the basis of identical twinning), and the cells left behind reorganize to replace what was removed, yielding a second normal embryo. (Choice B) is incorrect: each portion missing the other''s structures describes determinate cleavage, seen in protostomes, where fate is fixed early and removing a blastomere causes missing structures and failed development. (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly allows the isolated cell to develop but wrongly denies the compensating cells their regulative ability, which is exactly the property that keeps the remainder normal in indeterminate cleavage. (Choice D) is incorrect: losing cells at the cleavage stage is not fatal for deuterostomes; because commitment has not occurred, the remaining cells simply adjust rather than arrest. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of indeterminate cleavage to predict the developmental fate of both fragments, rather than recalling a single stated fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd29ef07a-3461-45f4-a0c6-e76c60e462c8';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd29ef07a-3461-45f4-a0c6-e76c60e462c8';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d29ef07a-3461-45f4-a0c6-e76c60e462c8', 'B', 'misconception', 'applies determinate (protostome) cleavage outcome to a deuterostome embryo'),
  ('d29ef07a-3461-45f4-a0c6-e76c60e462c8', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'keeps blastomere totipotency but denies the remainder''s compensating ability'),
  ('d29ef07a-3461-45f4-a0c6-e76c60e462c8', 'D', 'misconception', 'treats any cell loss during cleavage as fatal');

-- [Blastulation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During the cleavage stage, a researcher removes a single blastomere from an early sea urchin embryo. The remaining cells continue dividing and produce a complete, normally proportioned larva with no missing structures. Which conclusion about this embryo''s cleavage is best supported by the result?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The cleavage is indeterminate, so the remaining blastomeres were not yet committed to fixed fates."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The cleavage is determinate, so each blastomere''s developmental fate was already fixed early on."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The removed blastomere was totipotent, so its loss could not affect the rest of the embryo."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The embryo had already completed gastrulation, so its germ layers were fully established."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' During cleavage, the zygote divides into smaller cells called blastomeres, and species differ in whether those cells are already committed to specific fates. In embryos with indeterminate cleavage, the blastomeres are not yet fully committed, so if one is lost the neighboring cells can compensate and still form a complete embryo; in embryos with determinate cleavage, each cell''s fate is already fixed, so removing a blastomere leaves missing structures or a failed embryo. (Choice A) is correct: a normal larva forming after one blastomere is removed shows the remaining cells could take on the lost cell''s role, which is the defining behavior of indeterminate cleavage. (Choice B) is incorrect: determinate cleavage predicts the opposite outcome, because with fates already fixed the loss of a blastomere would produce a defective or nonviable embryo rather than a complete one. (Choice C) is incorrect: the observed result depends on what the remaining cells can do, not on a property of the cell that was removed, and calling the removed cell totipotent does not explain how the rest of the embryo replaced its contribution. (Choice D) is incorrect: cleavage precedes gastrulation, so a blastomere is removed well before germ layers form, and completed gastrulation would not explain the embryo''s ability to compensate for a missing cell. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of determinate versus indeterminate cleavage to a novel experimental result and infer the commitment state of the remaining blastomeres rather than recall a labeled fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a761a9d2-0cb6-492d-afd6-7886e29b3395';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a761a9d2-0cb6-492d-afd6-7886e29b3395';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a761a9d2-0cb6-492d-afd6-7886e29b3395', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'confusing determinate and indeterminate cleavage / opposite predicted outcome'),
  ('a761a9d2-0cb6-492d-afd6-7886e29b3395', 'C', 'misconception', 'attributing compensation to a property of the removed cell rather than the remaining cells'),
  ('a761a9d2-0cb6-492d-afd6-7886e29b3395', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'invoking a later developmental stage that does not apply during cleavage');

-- [Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes] ans=D hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'About five days after fertilization, a mammalian embryo reaches the uterus as a hollow ball of cells whose outer layer surrounds an off-center cluster called the inner cell mass. A researcher tags only the outer cell layer of this ball with a permanent fluorescent marker just before it attaches to the uterine lining. Several days later, after the embryo has burrowed into the endometrium, she examines the tissues. Where should the tagged cells appear?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Throughout the tissues of the embryo''s body, because the tagged outer layer is the population that later becomes the embryo proper."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Spread evenly across both the embryo''s body and the placenta, because the ball attaches to the lining before its cells commit to any layer."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Confined to the inner region that digs into the endometrium, because the inner cell mass is what invades the uterine wall during attachment."}, {"label": "D", "text": "In the tissue that invaded the endometrium and forms placental tissue, while the embryo''s own body remains untagged."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' The stage that implants in mammals is the blastocyst, whose cells are already arranged into two layers: an inner cell mass (the embryoblast) and an outer layer called the trophoblast. The textbook states that during implantation ''the outer layers of the zygote (blastocyst) grow into the endometrium by digesting the endometrial cells,'' and that in the blastocyst ''the inner cell mass... will go on to form the embryo,'' whereas ''the trophoblast will contribute to the placenta and nourish the embryo.'' A permanent tag on the outer layer therefore marks the trophoblast lineage, so its descendants should be found in the invading, placenta-forming tissue and not in the embryo''s own body. (Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the layer fates, because it is the inner cell mass, not the outer trophoblast, that gives rise to the embryo proper. (Choice B) is incorrect: by the time the blastocyst implants, its cells have already sorted into distinct inner-mass and trophoblast layers, so the tag does not distribute evenly across both embryo and placenta. (Choice C) is incorrect: it is the outer trophoblast layer, not the inner cell mass, that digests and burrows into the endometrium during implantation. (Choice D) is correct: the tagged outer layer is the trophoblast, which invades the endometrium and contributes to the placenta while the untagged inner cell mass forms the embryo. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the known fates of the blastocyst''s two layers to a lineage-tracing result and predict where the labeled cells will end up.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a5fbc634-9776-4583-8edf-a4c197ea00d2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a5fbc634-9776-4583-8edf-a4c197ea00d2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a5fbc634-9776-4583-8edf-a4c197ea00d2', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'swapped trophoblast and inner cell mass fates'),
  ('a5fbc634-9776-4583-8edf-a4c197ea00d2', 'B', 'misconception', 'blastocyst implants as an undifferentiated ball before layers form'),
  ('a5fbc634-9776-4583-8edf-a4c197ea00d2', 'C', 'misconception', 'inner cell mass mediates endometrial invasion during implantation');

-- [Neurulation & Neural Crest] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studying neurulation in a vertebrate embryo uses a fine needle to surgically remove the notochord from a small region along the dorsal midline before the neural plate has formed, leaving the overlying ectoderm and the neighboring germ layers intact. Development is then allowed to continue. What is the most likely outcome in the operated region?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The overlying ectoderm fails to organize a neural plate, and no neural tube forms in that region"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The overlying ectoderm forms a second, duplicated neural tube to compensate for the missing structure"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A normal neural tube still forms because the ectoderm rolls into a tube on its own schedule"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The underlying endoderm is recruited to build the neural tube in place of the missing notochord"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' During neurulation the neural tube—the precursor of the brain and spinal cord—arises from dorsal ectoderm, but that ectoderm does not fold on its own: the notochord, a mesodermal rod lying beneath it, releases signals that instruct the overlying cells to become the neural plate. The textbook states that in vertebrates the notochord "induces the development of the neural tube" and that the dorsal hollow nerve cord "is derived from ectoderm that rolls into a hollow tube during development" (Biology 2e, lines 36834, 36857). (Choice A) is correct: because the notochord provides the inductive signal that converts overlying ectoderm into the neural plate, removing it before induction leaves the ectoderm without its cue, so that stretch of ectoderm stays epidermal and no neural tube forms in the operated region. (Choice B) is incorrect: loss of the inducer produces a deficiency, not a compensatory duplication; a second, ectopic neural tube would instead be expected from adding an extra notochord, not from removing one. (Choice C) is incorrect: the ectoderm does not autonomously roll into a neural tube on a fixed schedule—neurulation depends on the notochord''s induction, so without that signal the folding does not occur. (Choice D) is incorrect: the neural tube is an ectodermal derivative, and the endoderm (which forms the digestive and respiratory linings) is neither the source of the neural tube nor a substitute inducer for the notochord. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of notochord-mediated neural induction to predict the result of an experimental perturbation the stem does not describe.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '5c59d324-ebf4-40f5-8219-20f9684add35';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '5c59d324-ebf4-40f5-8219-20f9684add35';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('5c59d324-ebf4-40f5-8219-20f9684add35', 'B', 'misconception', 'loss-of-signal triggers compensatory duplication'),
  ('5c59d324-ebf4-40f5-8219-20f9684add35', 'C', 'misconception', 'neural tube forms autonomously without induction'),
  ('5c59d324-ebf4-40f5-8219-20f9684add35', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'wrong germ layer substitutes as neural tube source');

-- [Twins] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A cleavage-stage human embryo is separated into two cell clusters, and each cluster goes on to develop into a complete, genetically identical individual, producing monozygotic twins. Which property of the embryo''s cells at the time of the split best accounts for the fact that each cluster can still form a whole individual?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The blastomeres have not yet become committed to specific fates, so each separated cell retains the potential to give rise to all parts of an embryo"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The blastomeres have already differentiated into distinct tissue precursors, which reorganize to rebuild the missing structures in each cluster"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The primary germ layers have already formed, so each cluster inherits a full set of endoderm, mesoderm, and ectoderm"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Two separate nuclei were present in the zygote from fertilization, and the split simply assigns one nucleus to each cluster"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' Cleavage is a series of mitotic divisions that subdivides the zygote into smaller cells called blastomeres without any cell growth. In humans and other deuterostomes, cleavage is indeterminate: early blastomeres are not yet committed to a particular fate, so removing or separating individual blastomeres does not cause the loss of structures, and each separated cluster can develop into a complete embryo. This uncommitted, still-potent state of the early cells is exactly what allows a single embryo to split and yield two whole, genetically identical individuals. (Choice A) is correct: because determination has not yet occurred at the cleavage stage, each blastomere still retains the developmental potential to build an entire embryo, so a separated cluster can regenerate all parts and form a complete twin. (Choice B) is incorrect: at the cleavage stage the blastomeres have not yet differentiated into tissue precursors; differentiation into specialized tissues comes later, and it is precisely the absence of early commitment—not reorganization of already-specialized cells—that permits each cluster to form a whole individual. (Choice C) is incorrect: the primary germ layers (endoderm, mesoderm, ectoderm) are not established until gastrulation, which occurs well after cleavage; a split relying on pre-formed germ layers describes a later, more committed embryo that could not readily give rise to two complete individuals. (Choice D) is incorrect: fertilization produces a single diploid zygote with one nucleus, and twinning results from separation of blastomeres during cleavage, not from partitioning of two nuclei that were somehow present from the start. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of cell determination to infer that the early embryo''s cells must be developmentally uncommitted for a split to yield two complete individuals.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '52ee2eb0-3eee-499f-abb0-402b7c2d888e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '52ee2eb0-3eee-499f-abb0-402b7c2d888e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('52ee2eb0-3eee-499f-abb0-402b7c2d888e', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes early blastomeres are already differentiated/specialized'),
  ('52ee2eb0-3eee-499f-abb0-402b7c2d888e', 'C', 'misconception', 'misplaces germ-layer formation to the cleavage stage instead of gastrulation'),
  ('52ee2eb0-3eee-499f-abb0-402b7c2d888e', 'D', 'misconception', 'confuses monozygotic twinning with a pre-existing two-nucleus zygote');

-- [Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A newborn with a severe inherited defect in the liver enzyme that clears bilirubin was completely healthy in utero, but bilirubin began rising in its blood only after birth. Assuming the enzyme was equally absent before and after delivery, which feature of the fetal period best explains why the defect produced no bilirubin buildup until after the baby was born?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The placenta let the parent''s blood carry fetal waste products away to be processed by the parent''s organs."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The fetal liver had not yet begun producing any enzymes, so no bilirubin was generated before birth."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The parent''s immunoglobulins crossing the placenta neutralized the excess bilirubin in the fetal blood."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fetal red blood cells do not break down before birth, so no bilirubin was produced until after delivery."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' During gestation the placenta takes over the nutrient and waste requirements of the fetus: the textbook states that with the placenta "the parent''s blood passing nutrients to the placenta and removing waste from it," and specifically that "chemicals from the fetus, such as bilirubin, are processed by the parent''s liver for elimination." A fetus whose own liver cannot clear a waste product can still remain healthy because the maternal circulation handles that clearance across the placenta; the burden shifts to the newborn only once the placental connection is severed at birth. (Choice A) is correct: because the placenta let the parent''s blood carry fetal waste (including bilirubin) to the parent''s liver for processing, the fetus''s own enzyme deficiency was masked, and the waste only accumulated once that maternal clearance was lost at birth. (Choice B) is incorrect: the fetal liver is active during gestation (it is one of the first organs to form and functions in the fetus), so bilirubin is indeed produced before birth — it is simply cleared by the parent, not by the fetal enzyme. (Choice C) is incorrect: maternal immunoglobulins do cross the placenta, but they provide passive immunity against infections and do not chemically neutralize a metabolic waste product like bilirubin. (Choice D) is incorrect: fetal red blood cells are continually broken down and replaced during gestation, so bilirubin is generated in the fetus; the reason it does not accumulate is placental clearance, not an absence of red-cell turnover. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that the placenta shifts fetal waste handling to the parent''s circulation to deduce why an inherited clearance defect stays silent until birth.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c29e68c1-7aae-4dcb-af67-dc5d17ac50f0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c29e68c1-7aae-4dcb-af67-dc5d17ac50f0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c29e68c1-7aae-4dcb-af67-dc5d17ac50f0', 'B', 'misconception', 'fetal organs are nonfunctional/absent until birth'),
  ('c29e68c1-7aae-4dcb-af67-dc5d17ac50f0', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'real placental function applied to wrong problem'),
  ('c29e68c1-7aae-4dcb-af67-dc5d17ac50f0', 'D', 'misconception', 'no fetal red-cell turnover before birth');

-- [Regeneration & Senescence] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A research team cultures four human cell types and measures telomere length at the start and after 30 rounds of division. For three of the cell types, the average telomere length is markedly shorter at round 30 than at the start. For one cell type, telomere length is essentially unchanged. Which cell type most likely showed no net loss of telomere length?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A dermal fibroblast taken from adult skin"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A terminally differentiated skeletal-muscle fiber"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A hematopoietic stem cell from adult bone marrow"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A mature keratinocyte from the epidermis"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Mechanisms of Development.'' Because DNA polymerase cannot replace the primer at the 5'' end of the lagging strand, the chromosome ends (telomeres) shorten a little with each division unless the enzyme telomerase rebuilds them; the text states that telomerase "is typically active in germ cells and adult stem cells" but "is not active in adult somatic cells," so ordinary dividing somatic cells progressively lose telomere length. Identifying the cell whose telomeres stay constant means finding the one type that actually expresses telomerase. (Choice A) is incorrect: a dermal fibroblast is a differentiated adult somatic cell, which does not make telomerase, so its telomeres shorten with each round of division. (Choice B) is incorrect: a skeletal-muscle fiber is a terminally differentiated somatic cell and likewise lacks telomerase activity; being long-lived or highly specialized does not confer telomere maintenance. (Choice C) is correct: an adult bone-marrow hematopoietic stem cell is an adult stem cell, one of the cell types in which telomerase is normally active, so it can add telomeric repeats and keep telomere length essentially constant across divisions. (Choice D) is incorrect: a mature keratinocyte is a differentiated epidermal somatic cell that does not express telomerase, so its telomeres also erode with repeated division. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that telomerase activity is restricted to germ cells and adult stem cells in order to predict which cell type resists telomere shortening in the experiment, rather than merely recalling what telomerase does.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '08275350-6668-45f8-ad07-3f9bccf4eaca';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '08275350-6668-45f8-ad07-3f9bccf4eaca';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('08275350-6668-45f8-ad07-3f9bccf4eaca', 'A', 'misconception', 'somatic cell assumed to retain telomerase'),
  ('08275350-6668-45f8-ad07-3f9bccf4eaca', 'B', 'misconception', 'specialization/longevity confused with telomere maintenance'),
  ('08275350-6668-45f8-ad07-3f9bccf4eaca', 'D', 'misconception', 'differentiated somatic cell assumed to express telomerase');

-- [Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During gastrulation in a triploblastic embryo, a localized signaling error selectively impairs the cells of one primary germ layer, the mesoderm, while the other germ layers develop normally. Assuming the affected cells fail to contribute to their normal derivatives, which set of adult structures would be most directly compromised?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The skeleton, the circulating blood cells, and the heart muscle"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The lining of the intestines, the lining of the lungs, and the liver"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The brain, the spinal cord, and the epidermis of the skin"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The lining of the trachea, the epidermis, and the lining of the stomach"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Embryogenesis.'' During gastrulation the three primary germ layers are established, and each is programmed to give rise to a specific set of tissues and organs, so a defect confined to one layer would compromise only that layer''s derivatives. The textbook states that the mesoderm gives rise to all specialized muscle tissues (including cardiac muscle), connective tissues such as the skeleton and blood cells, and most visceral organs such as the kidneys and spleen; the endoderm forms the lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts (including the liver, pancreas, and lungs); and the ectoderm forms the outer epithelial covering of the body and the central nervous system. (Choice A) is correct: the skeleton, blood cells, and heart (cardiac) muscle are all connective and muscular tissues that arise from mesoderm, so all three would be directly affected by a mesodermal defect. (Choice B) is incorrect: the lining of the intestines, the lining of the lungs, and the liver are all endodermal derivatives, so they would be spared when only the mesoderm is impaired. (Choice C) is incorrect: the brain, spinal cord, and epidermis are all ectodermal derivatives, a common error being to assign the central nervous system to mesoderm rather than to ectoderm. (Choice D) is incorrect: the epidermis is ectodermal while the linings of the trachea and stomach are endodermal, so this mixed set includes no mesodermal structures and would not be primarily compromised. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the germ-layer origins of specific tissues to determine which structures share a common mesodermal derivation and would therefore be co-affected by a single-layer defect.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '6acc9ace-1f2c-49b3-b026-01922b1cf48b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6acc9ace-1f2c-49b3-b026-01922b1cf48b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6acc9ace-1f2c-49b3-b026-01922b1cf48b', 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong-germ-layer-assignment'),
  ('6acc9ace-1f2c-49b3-b026-01922b1cf48b', 'C', 'misconception', 'cns-as-mesoderm'),
  ('6acc9ace-1f2c-49b3-b026-01922b1cf48b', 'D', 'misconception', 'mixed-layer-set');

-- [Endoderm Derivatives] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studying a teratogen finds that exposed embryos develop malformations in three distinct tissues: the epithelial lining of the intestines, the glandular epithelium of the pancreas, and the parenchyma of the liver. The surrounding smooth muscle of the gut wall and the associated blood vessels appear normal. The malformations are best explained by disruption of a single primary germ layer. Which germ layer''s derivatives were affected?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Ectoderm"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Mesoderm"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Endoderm"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Neural crest"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' During gastrulation, the three primary germ layers are established, and each is programmed to give rise to specific tissues: the endoderm forms the lining of the digestive tract (including the stomach, intestines, liver, and pancreas) and the lining of the respiratory tract; the ectoderm forms the epidermis and nervous system; and the mesoderm forms muscle, connective tissue, blood, and most visceral organs. The key inference here is that the three affected tissues — intestinal epithelium, pancreatic glandular epithelium, and liver parenchyma — all share a common endodermal origin, whereas the spared smooth muscle and blood vessels are mesodermal. (Choice A) is incorrect: the ectoderm gives rise to the outer epithelial covering of the body surface and the central nervous system, not the epithelia and parenchyma of the gut-derived digestive organs described here. (Choice B) is incorrect: the mesoderm forms muscle, connective tissue, and blood vessels; because the smooth muscle of the gut wall and the blood vessels were spared, mesoderm is not the disrupted layer. (Choice C) is correct: the intestinal lining, pancreatic epithelium, and liver all derive from endoderm, so a single insult to the endoderm accounts for all three malformations while sparing the mesodermal muscle and vessels. (Choice D) is incorrect: neural crest is a specialized migratory population arising from the ectodermal region and contributes to peripheral neurons, melanocytes, and craniofacial cartilage, none of which are the gut epithelia and hepatic parenchyma affected here. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your knowledge of the derivatives of each germ layer to a novel scenario and infer that only a shared endodermal origin explains the specific pattern of affected and spared tissues.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a14b9129-eeca-4a94-b876-4e1ea2871939';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a14b9129-eeca-4a94-b876-4e1ea2871939';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a14b9129-eeca-4a94-b876-4e1ea2871939', 'A', 'misconception', 'wrong-germ-layer-derivative'),
  ('a14b9129-eeca-4a94-b876-4e1ea2871939', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'spared-tissue-confused-for-source'),
  ('a14b9129-eeca-4a94-b876-4e1ea2871939', 'D', 'misconception', 'fourth-lineage-lure');

-- [Endoderm Derivatives] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher labels a population of endodermal cells in an early triploblastic embryo with a permanent fluorescent marker and later examines an adult tissue section of the intestinal wall, which contains an inner epithelial lining, a supporting connective-tissue layer, and surrounding smooth muscle. Which component of this intestinal wall should show the fluorescent label?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The smooth muscle layers that propel material through the intestine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The connective tissue that supports and separates the wall''s layers"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The epithelium that lines the interior surface of the intestinal tube"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The blood cells within the small vessels running through the wall"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' Each primary germ layer is programmed to give rise to specific tissues: the textbook states that the endoderm gives rise to the lining of the digestive tract (including the stomach, intestines, liver, and pancreas) and the lining of the respiratory tract, whereas the mesoderm gives rise to all specialized muscle tissues (including the muscles of the intestines), connective tissues such as the skeleton and blood cells, and most visceral organs. A single organ like the intestine is therefore built from more than one germ layer, so the label tracks the tissue type, not the organ as a whole. (Choice A) is incorrect: the smooth muscle that moves material through the intestine is a specialized muscle tissue, which the text explicitly assigns to the mesoderm, so it would not carry an endodermal label. (Choice B) is incorrect: connective tissue is derived from the mesoderm, so the supporting connective-tissue layer of the intestinal wall arises from mesoderm rather than endoderm. (Choice C) is correct: the epithelium lining the interior surface of the digestive tube is exactly the derivative the text assigns to the endoderm, so this layer would retain the endodermal fluorescent marker. (Choice D) is incorrect: blood cells are listed among the connective-tissue derivatives of the mesoderm, so the blood cells within the wall''s vessels would not be endodermal. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the germ-layer-to-tissue assignment rules to distinguish which specific tissue within a multi-layered organ traces to the endoderm rather than treating the whole organ as one lineage.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '09f74e63-8b6b-4a9f-a144-037ba31a7ad0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '09f74e63-8b6b-4a9f-a144-037ba31a7ad0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('09f74e63-8b6b-4a9f-a144-037ba31a7ad0', 'A', 'misconception', 'whole-organ-one-layer'),
  ('09f74e63-8b6b-4a9f-a144-037ba31a7ad0', 'B', 'misconception', 'layer-derivative-confusion'),
  ('09f74e63-8b6b-4a9f-a144-037ba31a7ad0', 'D', 'misconception', 'layer-derivative-confusion');

-- [Twins] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A developmental biologist images a single sea urchin embryo continuously from the moment of fertilization. She records the embryo first as a single-celled zygote and again once it has reached the hollow blastula stage, several rounds of division later. Compared with the original zygote, what should she observe about the blastula?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "About the same total size as the zygote, but subdivided into many smaller cells."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Many times larger than the zygote, having added new cytoplasm with each division."}, {"label": "C", "text": "About the same total size as the zygote, and made of cells each as large as the zygote."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Noticeably smaller than the zygote, its cytoplasm partly consumed to fuel each division."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' Animal development begins with cleavage, a rapid series of mitotic divisions of the zygote. The OpenStax Biology 2e text notes that cleavage "differs from somatic cell division in that the egg is subdivided by successive cleavages into smaller and smaller cells, with no actual cell growth," so that "the 8-cell stage and the blastula are about the same size as the original zygote." In other words, cleavage increases cell number and decreases cell size while the total mass of the embryo stays essentially constant. (Choice A) is correct: because there is no net cell growth during cleavage, the blastula occupies roughly the same overall volume as the zygote but is now partitioned into many much smaller blastomeres. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reflects the common misconception that cell division equals growth; cleavage adds no new cytoplasm, so the embryo does not enlarge, and appreciable growth of the embryo does not occur until after implantation and later development. (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly keeps total size constant but wrongly assumes the daughter cells remain zygote-sized, which is geometrically impossible—if many cells fit within the original volume, each blastomere must be far smaller than the zygote. (Choice D) is incorrect: although mitosis does consume energy, cleavage does not measurably shrink the embryo, because the cytoplasm is repartitioned among blastomeres rather than being burned away, so the blastula is not smaller than the zygote. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining property of cleavage—division without net growth—to predict how an embryo''s total size and cell size change between the zygote and blastula stages.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '9da6232e-4c68-4ed2-807e-88c5dfbe03f6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9da6232e-4c68-4ed2-807e-88c5dfbe03f6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9da6232e-4c68-4ed2-807e-88c5dfbe03f6', 'B', 'misconception', 'division equals growth'),
  ('9da6232e-4c68-4ed2-807e-88c5dfbe03f6', 'C', 'misconception', 'constant cell size despite division'),
  ('9da6232e-4c68-4ed2-807e-88c5dfbe03f6', 'D', 'misconception', 'cytoplasm consumed as fuel');

-- [Gestation & Birth] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During labor in a healthy full-term pregnancy, the descending fetal head presses against and stretches the cervix. This stretch triggers nerve impulses to the hypothalamus that cause the posterior pituitary to release oxytocin, which drives uterine smooth muscle to contract. As labor progresses toward delivery, what does this arrangement predict will happen to the strength of uterine contractions, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Contractions grow progressively stronger, because each contraction pushes the fetus harder against the cervix, increasing stretch and oxytocin release"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Contractions stabilize near a set point, because rising oxytocin is sensed and its further release is inhibited to keep contraction force constant"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Contractions weaken over time, because each contraction pushes the fetus away from the cervix, reducing stretch and oxytocin release"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Contractions plateau early, because the uterine muscle becomes progressively less responsive to oxytocin as contractions continue"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' Parturition is one of the few positive feedback loops in the human body: cervical stretch signals the hypothalamus, the posterior pituitary releases oxytocin, and oxytocin contracts uterine smooth muscle. A positive feedback loop maintains and amplifies the original stimulus rather than opposing it, so the key is to trace whether the output feeds back to increase or decrease the triggering signal. (Choice A) is correct: the textbook describes a positive feedback relay in which uterine contractions drive the fetus harder against the cervix, causing more stretch, which recruits still more oxytocin, so "the contractions increase in intensity and force" until birth is complete. (Choice B) is incorrect: inhibiting oxytocin release to hold contraction force at a set point describes negative feedback, the mechanism that predominates in homeostasis (as with insulin and blood glucose), but labor is explicitly one of the rare positive feedback loops that escalates rather than stabilizes. (Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the geometry of the loop—contractions push the fetal head toward and into the cervix, increasing stretch, not away from it, so the signal is amplified rather than diminished. (Choice D) is incorrect: uterine sensitivity to oxytocin actually rises before labor as estrogen increases oxytocin receptor number late in pregnancy; the muscle does not become refractory mid-labor, and declining responsiveness would not explain the observed escalation of contractions. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of a positive feedback loop to a described signaling pathway to predict how contraction strength changes over the course of labor.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '19f81735-b461-4eb2-bc4b-98850fecd6a6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '19f81735-b461-4eb2-bc4b-98850fecd6a6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('19f81735-b461-4eb2-bc4b-98850fecd6a6', 'B', 'misconception', 'assuming all physiological feedback is negative/homeostatic'),
  ('19f81735-b461-4eb2-bc4b-98850fecd6a6', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'reversing the direction of the feedback signal'),
  ('19f81735-b461-4eb2-bc4b-98850fecd6a6', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'confusing rising pre-labor oxytocin sensitivity with mid-labor desensitization');

-- [Cell Migration & Apoptosis] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Normal epithelial cells carry receptors that bind the extracellular matrix (ECM); this binding sustains a signaling cascade that keeps the cell alive. A researcher isolates a mutant cell line that continues to survive and divide even after it is fully detached from any ECM. Compared with normal cells, this mutant line is MOST likely to display which of the following behaviors?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Increased ability to migrate through the body and proliferate at distant sites"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Faster onset of programmed cell death whenever the cell loses contact with the matrix"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Reduced rate of cell division because the survival signal is no longer received"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Greater dependence on matrix attachment to complete each round of the cell cycle"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Mechanisms of Development.'' OpenStax Biology 2e explains that most normal animal cells have receptors that bind the extracellular matrix, and this binding initiates a survival signaling cascade; if a cell moves away from the matrix, the signaling ceases and the cell undergoes apoptosis. The text notes that this system "keeps cells from traveling through the body and proliferating out of control, as happens with tumor cells that metastasize." A mutant that survives detachment has lost this safeguard. (Choice A) is correct: because the mutant no longer dies when it separates from the matrix, it can survive while wandering away from its original tissue and proliferate at distant sites — exactly the anchorage-independent behavior characteristic of metastatic tumor cells. (Choice B) is incorrect: the mutant shows the opposite of faster death on detachment; the whole point is that it fails to trigger apoptosis when it loses matrix contact. (Choice C) is incorrect: the mutant divides even without the matrix signal, so its division is not reduced by loss of that signal — it has become independent of it, not more reliant on it. (Choice D) is incorrect: the mutant is less dependent, not more dependent, on matrix attachment; it completes the cell cycle without the anchorage-derived survival signal that normal cells require. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept that ECM-dependent survival signaling normally triggers apoptosis in detached cells to predict how losing that safeguard enables uncontrolled migration and metastasis.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2bd41320-bdd3-48de-97d1-dfb2079b6400';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2bd41320-bdd3-48de-97d1-dfb2079b6400';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2bd41320-bdd3-48de-97d1-dfb2079b6400', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'inverts the mutant''s phenotype (resists death) into its opposite (dies faster)'),
  ('2bd41320-bdd3-48de-97d1-dfb2079b6400', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes losing the survival signal must slow division rather than being bypassed'),
  ('2bd41320-bdd3-48de-97d1-dfb2079b6400', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'states increased anchorage dependence when the mutant is anchorage-independent');

-- [Fertilization] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During in vitro fertilization of a sea urchin egg, a researcher uses a voltage clamp to hold the egg''s membrane potential fixed at its resting value, preventing the rapid electrical change that normally occurs the instant the first sperm fuses. The structural remodeling of the egg surface that follows is left undisturbed. Sperm are then added, and the researcher records how many sperm fuse with the egg over the next several minutes.',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "No sperm can fuse at all, because holding the membrane potential fixed permanently seals the egg surface to every sperm."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A few extra sperm fuse immediately after the first, but further fusion is then prevented as the egg surface remodels."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Sperm continue fusing indefinitely, because eliminating the electrical change removes the egg''s only barrier to additional sperm."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Exactly one sperm fuses on schedule, because the electrical change plays no role in limiting how many sperm enter."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' The egg guards against polyspermy with two mechanisms that act on different timescales: a fast block, in which the membrane potential changes almost instantly after the first sperm fuses (Ernest Everett Just''s "wave of negativity"), and a slower block, in which the membrane structure itself is physically remodeled. Because these are separate mechanisms, disabling one does not disable the other. (Choice B) is correct: clamping the membrane potential removes only the fast electrical block, so during the brief window before the surface finishes remodeling a few additional sperm can transiently fuse; once the slower structural block develops, further fusion is prevented, so the egg is not left permanently open. (Choice A) is incorrect: fixing the membrane potential does not seal the egg — it removes the electrical barrier rather than reinforcing it, so early fusion is made more likely, not impossible. (Choice C) is incorrect: it treats the electrical change as the egg''s only defense, but the independent structural remodeling still develops and eventually halts fusion, so sperm entry is not unlimited. (Choice D) is incorrect: the electrical change does contribute to limiting sperm entry, so removing it while leaving the slow block intact still perturbs the early response rather than leaving the normal one-sperm outcome untouched. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between the fast (membrane-potential) block and the slow (structural) block to predict the outcome of selectively disabling only the fast block.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2225245e-2be9-4010-8e0a-dcd8452072cf';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2225245e-2be9-4010-8e0a-dcd8452072cf';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2225245e-2be9-4010-8e0a-dcd8452072cf', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'confusing removal of a barrier with reinforcement of it'),
  ('2225245e-2be9-4010-8e0a-dcd8452072cf', 'C', 'misconception', 'treating the fast electrical block as the egg''s sole anti-polyspermy defense'),
  ('2225245e-2be9-4010-8e0a-dcd8452072cf', 'D', 'misconception', 'believing the membrane-potential change is irrelevant to blocking polyspermy');

-- [Ectoderm Derivatives] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a vertebrate embryo, cells at the edge of the ectoderm normally respond to a diffusible growth-factor signal, while cells in the center of the ectoderm do not receive it. A researcher engineers embryos in which this growth-factor signal is completely eliminated across the entire ectoderm, then examines the ectodermal derivatives that form. Which outcome is most likely?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The entire ectoderm forms neural tissue, and no epidermis develops."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The entire ectoderm forms epidermis, and no neural tissue develops."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The ectoderm forms both epidermis and neural tissue in their normal proportions."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The ectoderm forms epidermis, while the neural tissue arises instead from the underlying mesoderm."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' During organogenesis, the ectoderm gives rise to both the outer epidermis and the neural tissue of the central nervous system, but these are not equivalent default fates: the OpenStax text states that "special signaling molecules called growth factors signal some cells at the edge of the ectoderm to become epidermis cells" while "the remaining cells in the center form the neural plate," and critically that "if the signaling by growth factors were disrupted, then the entire ectoderm would differentiate into neural tissue." In other words, neural is the default ectodermal fate, and an active growth-factor signal is required to divert edge cells into the epidermal pathway. (Choice A) is correct: removing the growth-factor signal from the entire ectoderm removes the only cue that produces epidermis, so all ectodermal cells follow the default neural pathway and no epidermis forms. (Choice B) is incorrect because it reverses the roles of signal and default; epidermis is the induced fate that requires the growth-factor signal, so eliminating the signal abolishes epidermis rather than producing it everywhere. (Choice C) is incorrect because normal proportions of epidermis and neural tissue depend on the spatial restriction of the growth-factor signal to edge cells; with the signal gone entirely, the balance collapses toward neural tissue rather than remaining unchanged. (Choice D) is incorrect because neural tissue is an ectodermal derivative, not a mesodermal one—the mesoderm flanking the neural tube gives rise to connective tissues, somites, and the notochord, not to the neural plate. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that neural tissue is the default ectodermal fate and epidermis is the signal-induced fate to predict the outcome of removing that inductive signal.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '710e4769-213d-415f-ab80-c13bbb7d8fb5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '710e4769-213d-415f-ab80-c13bbb7d8fb5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('710e4769-213d-415f-ab80-c13bbb7d8fb5', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps default fate with induced fate'),
  ('710e4769-213d-415f-ab80-c13bbb7d8fb5', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes signal loss has no effect on fate proportions'),
  ('710e4769-213d-415f-ab80-c13bbb7d8fb5', 'D', 'misconception', 'misassigns neural tissue to mesoderm instead of ectoderm');

-- [Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In an in vitro study, a human blastocyst reaches a prepared uterine lining but never burrows into it: its outer cell layer fails to digest and grow into the endometrium, so no placenta ever forms. When the internal cluster of cells from the same blastocyst is instead transferred on its own, it differentiates normally into embryonic tissues. A defect in which blastocyst structure best accounts for this pattern?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The trophoblast"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The inner cell mass"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The zona pellucida"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The endometrium"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' When the mammalian blastula becomes a blastocyst, its cells arrange into two populations: an inner cell mass (embryoblast) and an outer layer called the trophoblast. The inner cell mass consists of embryonic stem cells that go on to form the embryo, whereas the trophoblast grows into the endometrium during implantation and contributes to the placenta. (Choice A) is correct: the described defect is a failure of the outer layer to invade the endometrium and form a placenta, and the trophoblast is precisely the layer that, during implantation, digests the endometrial cells and becomes the fetal portion of the placenta; a trophoblast defect therefore blocks implantation while leaving the embryo-forming cells intact. (Choice B) is incorrect: the inner cell mass differentiates into the embryo proper, not the placenta, and the scenario states these internal cells develop normally when transferred, so they are functioning rather than defective. (Choice C) is incorrect: the zona pellucida is the glycoprotein coat surrounding the oocyte that must be shed ("hatched") before implantation; its loss would not explain a failure of the outer cells to invade an already-accessible endometrium, and it is not the structure that forms the placenta. (Choice D) is incorrect: the endometrium is the maternal uterine lining, not a part of the blastocyst, and the scenario specifies it was prepared and receptive, so the failure lies in the embryonic tissue that should have invaded it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinct fates of the inner cell mass and trophoblast to a loss-of-function scenario and trace an implantation/placental defect to the specific blastocyst layer responsible.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '985b6f45-cd9c-4fd7-b0d3-4814ad55e76d';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '985b6f45-cd9c-4fd7-b0d3-4814ad55e76d';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('985b6f45-cd9c-4fd7-b0d3-4814ad55e76d', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swapping the fates of the inner cell mass and trophoblast'),
  ('985b6f45-cd9c-4fd7-b0d3-4814ad55e76d', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'invoking a pre-implantation structure (zona pellucida) unrelated to placenta formation'),
  ('985b6f45-cd9c-4fd7-b0d3-4814ad55e76d', 'D', 'misconception', 'confusing the maternal endometrium with an embryonic blastocyst layer');

-- [Ectoderm Derivatives] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During neurulation in a vertebrate embryo, growth factors normally released at the outer margins of the ectoderm instruct the marginal cells to adopt one fate while the cells in the center adopt another. In a mutant embryo, these growth factors fail to be produced, so no ectodermal cell ever receives the margin signal. Assuming the ectoderm still develops, what tissue is this germ layer now most likely to form across essentially its entire extent?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Neural tissue, with little or no surface epidermis produced from the ectoderm"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A thickened surface epidermis, with little or no neural tissue produced from the ectoderm"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Segmented somites that give rise to vertebrae, ribs, and skeletal muscle"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Columnar epithelium that lines the digestive tract and its associated organs"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' During organogenesis, the ectoderm gives rise to two things: the epidermis and the nervous system. The textbook specifies that growth factors signal the cells at the edge of the ectoderm to become epidermis, while the remaining cells in the center form the neural plate, which rolls into the neural tube and becomes the brain and spinal cord; critically, it notes that if this growth-factor signaling were disrupted, the entire ectoderm would differentiate into neural tissue. (Choice A) is correct: because the epidermis is the induced fate that requires the margin growth-factor signal, removing that signal leaves neural tissue as the default fate, so nearly the whole ectoderm differentiates into neural tissue with little or no epidermis. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the induction logic — epidermis is the fate that depends on the growth factor, so losing the signal reduces epidermis rather than expanding it, and neural tissue is the pathway that proceeds without it. (Choice C) is incorrect: somites, vertebrae, ribs, and skeletal muscle are mesodermal derivatives, not ectodermal, so no ectodermal manipulation redirects this layer into somites. (Choice D) is incorrect: the columnar epithelium lining the digestive tract and internal organs derives from endoderm, a different germ layer that the ectoderm does not switch into. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that epidermis is the growth-factor-induced fate and neural tissue is the default fate to predict the outcome of removing the inducing signal.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'de4796ef-9376-4a23-aa90-6228591e6106';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'de4796ef-9376-4a23-aa90-6228591e6106';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('de4796ef-9376-4a23-aa90-6228591e6106', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'signal-loss removes derivative instead of revealing default fate'),
  ('de4796ef-9376-4a23-aa90-6228591e6106', 'C', 'misconception', 'germ-layer derivative confusion (mesoderm assigned to ectoderm)'),
  ('de4796ef-9376-4a23-aa90-6228591e6106', 'D', 'misconception', 'germ-layer derivative confusion (endoderm assigned to ectoderm)');

-- [Fertilization] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a fertilization experiment, a drug prevents the rapid change in a sea urchin egg''s membrane potential that normally occurs the instant the first sperm fuses with it. All other events proceed. Compared with an untreated egg, what is the most likely outcome for the treated egg after it is exposed to sperm?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Several sperm fuse with the egg, and the embryo is nonviable because it carries more than a single diploid set of chromosomes."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Several sperm fuse with the egg, but the embryo develops normally because only the first sperm nucleus joins with the egg nucleus."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Only one sperm fuses with the egg because the first sperm''s acrosomal enzymes have already destroyed the egg''s outer coat."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Several sperm fuse with the egg, but the embryo is viable and haploid because the surplus sperm genomes are broken down."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' Fertilization must combine exactly one haploid sperm genome with one haploid egg genome to produce a single diploid zygote, so the egg has mechanisms to block additional sperm once the first has fused. The text describes a fast response in which "the membrane potential of the egg cell altered quickly" followed by a slow structural change, and warns that if "these mechanisms fail, multiple sperm can fuse with the egg, resulting in polyspermy" — a condition in which "the resulting embryo is not genetically viable and dies within a few days." (Choice A) is correct: removing the rapid membrane-potential change disables the fast block to polyspermy, so several sperm fuse and each contributes a genome, giving the embryo extra chromosome sets rather than a single diploid set, which is lethal. (Choice B) is incorrect: polyspermy is not harmless — each fused sperm delivers its nucleus, so the embryo does not simply use only the first sperm''s genome and develop normally. (Choice C) is incorrect: it is the egg''s own fast and slow blocks, not the acrosomal reaction, that prevent additional sperm from fusing; the acrosomal enzymes help a sperm penetrate the coat rather than seal it against other sperm. (Choice D) is incorrect: the surplus genomes are not selectively degraded to leave a haploid embryo, and a viable embryo requires the diploid condition, not a haploid one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the function of the block to polyspermy and the requirement for a single diploid genome to predict the outcome when the fast block is disabled.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '0cf406c3-312f-413d-9914-f1608df6787b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0cf406c3-312f-413d-9914-f1608df6787b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0cf406c3-312f-413d-9914-f1608df6787b', 'B', 'misconception', 'harmless_extra_sperm'),
  ('0cf406c3-312f-413d-9914-f1608df6787b', 'C', 'misconception', 'acrosome_prevents_polyspermy'),
  ('0cf406c3-312f-413d-9914-f1608df6787b', 'D', 'misconception', 'surplus_genomes_degraded');

-- [Blastulation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studying mammalian development injects a permanent fluorescent lineage tracer into the outer layer of cells of an early blastocyst, leaving the inner cluster of cells unlabeled. The embryo is then allowed to implant and develop normally. When the researcher later examines the resulting conceptus, which distribution of the fluorescent label is most consistent with the fates of the cell populations in the blastocyst?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The label is found in the placenta and supporting extraembryonic tissue but is largely absent from the fetus itself."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The label is found throughout the tissues of the fetus but is largely absent from the placenta."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The label is distributed about evenly between the tissues of the fetus and the placenta."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The label is confined to the fluid-filled cavity and disappears once that cavity is obliterated."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' In mammals, the blastula reorganizes into the blastocyst, in which the cells separate into two distinct populations with different fates: an inner cell mass and an outer layer called the trophoblast. The textbook states that the inner cell mass "will go on to form the embryo," consisting of "embryonic stem cells that will differentiate into the different cell types needed by the organism," while "the trophoblast will contribute to the placenta and nourish the embryo." (Choice A) is correct: because the tracer is placed in the outer layer (the trophoblast), its descendants populate the placenta and extraembryonic supporting tissue rather than the body of the fetus, so the fetus itself is largely unlabeled. (Choice B) is incorrect: labeling of the fetal tissues but not the placenta is the pattern expected if the tracer had been placed in the inner cell mass, which forms the embryo proper, not in the outer trophoblast layer that was actually injected. (Choice C) is incorrect: an even split between fetus and placenta would imply the blastocyst''s cells are still equivalent and can contribute equally to both, but by the blastocyst stage the inner cell mass and trophoblast are already committed to separate embryonic versus extraembryonic fates. (Choice D) is incorrect: the outer cells do not simply line the blastocoel and vanish with it; the trophoblast is a lasting cell population that goes on to form part of the placenta. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinct developmental fates of the trophoblast and inner cell mass to predict where a lineage label placed in the outer blastocyst layer will end up.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
