-- [Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A developmental biologist fluorescently labels a small population of cells in an early triploblastic embryo and, over the following days, watches the labeled cells contribute to the muscular wall of the developing heart. To predict what else this labeled population will build, the biologist wants to know which additional adult structure should arise from these same cells.',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The lining of the small intestine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The epidermis of the skin"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The cortex of the kidney"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The neurons of the central nervous system"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question about the major structures that arise from the primary germ layers established during embryogenesis. During embryogenesis, the three primary germ layers established at gastrulation are each programmed to give rise to a characteristic set of tissues and organs, so identifying the layer that produces one structure lets you predict its other derivatives. Cells that form the muscular wall of the heart belong to the mesoderm, because the text states this germ layer "gives rise to all specialized muscle tissues (including the cardiac tissues and muscles of the intestines), connective tissues such as the skeleton and blood cells, and most other visceral organs such as the kidneys and the spleen." (Choice A) is incorrect: the lining of the digestive tract, including the small intestine, is an endodermal derivative, as the text lists the endoderm as giving rise to "the lining of the digestive tract (including the stomach, intestines, liver, and pancreas)," a different germ layer from the one that forms cardiac muscle. (Choice B) is incorrect: the epidermis is ectodermal, since the ectoderm "develops into the outer epithelial covering of the body surface," so it does not share an origin with the heart muscle. (Choice C) is correct: the kidney is explicitly named among the visceral organs derived from mesoderm, the same germ layer that forms cardiac muscle, so the labeled cardiac-forming population should also be able to build kidney tissue. (Choice D) is incorrect: the central nervous system arises from ectoderm, which the text says develops into "the central nervous system," making it a different lineage from the mesodermal heart cells. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that germ-layer derivatives cluster by lineage, inferring the mesodermal identity of the labeled cells from one product (cardiac muscle) and then predicting a second product (kidney) from that same layer.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'e999868d-071d-4780-8144-c794e95cdeb1';
